tick: 978
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{read 89}\n/execute_action{read 83}\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"MR–SelfTune Graph PCA (ridge): k=50, kd=22, δ=0.10, nPC=100 (seed=0)\"\ntags: \"graph, pca, density-scaling, mutual, sparse\"\nabstract: \"Implements a parsimonious GraphFWPCA variant with self-tuned local scaling and soft reciprocal weighting. We build a kNN graph on 100-PC space from log-normalized counts, scale distances by the kd-th neighbor (kd=22) with exponent δ=0.10, and convert to weighted connectivities via 1/(eps+scaled_distance), then symmetrize with reciprocal-boost (soft mutual). Parameters follow Praxis ridge; returns both weighted connectivities and distances.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.decomposition import TruncatedSVD\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n  import anndata as ad\n\n  # ---- Utilities ----\n\n  def _row_scale_csr(X, scale):\n      \"\"\"Multiply CSR matrix\
    \ rows by scale (1D array length n_rows).\"\"\"\n      if not sp.issparse(X):\n          return (X.T * scale).T\n      if not sp.isspmatrix_csr(X):\n          X = X.tocsr()\n      X = X.copy()\n      X.data *= np.repeat(scale, np.diff(X.indptr))\n      return X\n\n  def _log1p_csr_inplace(X):\n      if not sp.isspmatrix_csr(X):\n          X = X.tocsr()\n      X = X.copy()\n      X.data = np.log1p(X.data)\n      return X\n\n  def _to_csr(X):\n      if sp.isspmatrix_csr(X):\n          return X\n      if sp.issparse(X):\n          return X.tocsr()\n      return sp.csr_matrix(X)\n\n  # ---- Core algorithm ----\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_from_counts(adata):\n      \"\"\"\n      Normalize from raw counts (layers['counts'] if present else X):\n      - library size normalize to 1e4\n      - log1p\n      Returns sparse CSR matrix (n_cells x n_genes).\n      \"\"\"\n      Xc = adata.layers['counts'] if 'counts' in adata.layers else adata.X\n      Xc = _to_csr(Xc).astype(np.float32)\n      lib =\
    \ np.asarray(Xc.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n      lib = np.maximum(lib, 1e-9)\n      scale = (1e4 / lib).astype(np.float32)\n      Xn = _row_scale_csr(Xc, scale)\n      Xn = _log1p_csr_inplace(Xn)\n      return Xn\n\n  def _select_features(X, adata):\n      \"\"\"\n      Select features using batch-aware HVG if available; otherwise, keep all.\n      X: CSR (n x g)\n      \"\"\"\n      if 'batch_hvg' in adata.var.columns:\n          mask = adata.var['batch_hvg'].values.astype(bool)\n          if mask.sum() > 0 and mask.sum() < X.shape[1]:\n              return X[:, mask]\n      return X\n\n  def _pca_truncated(X, n_components=100, random_state=0):\n      \"\"\"\n      PCA via TruncatedSVD for sparse input (no centering). Returns dense np.ndarray.\n      \"\"\"\n      # Cap n_components to min(n_samples-1, n_features)\n      n_cells, n_genes = X.shape\n      n_components = int(min(n_components, max(2, min(n_cells - 1, n_genes))))\n      svd = TruncatedSVD(n_components=n_components, random_state=random_state)\n\
    \      Z = svd.fit_transform(X)\n      return Z.astype(np.float32)\n\n  def _build_knn(Z, k=50, kd=22, delta=0.10, extra=10, n_jobs=-1):\n      \"\"\"\n      Build kNN on Z with self-tuned local scaling by kd-th neighbor and soft reciprocal boost.\n      Returns: (connectivities CSR, distances CSR)\n      \"\"\"\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      k_query = int(max(k, kd) + extra)\n      # Fit NN (include self neighbor; sklearn returns self at first position)\n      nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_query + 1, algorithm='auto', metric='euclidean', n_jobs=n_jobs)\n      nn.fit(Z)\n      d_all, idx_all = nn.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n      # Drop self\n      d = d_all[:, 1:]\n      idx = idx_all[:, 1:]\n\n      # Local scale by kd-th neighbor (1-based indexing becomes kd position here)\n      kd_pos = int(min(kd, d.shape[1])) - 1\n      sigma = d[:, kd_pos]  # shape (n,)\n      sigma = np.maximum(sigma, 1e-12)\n      # Scaled distances: divide by mean(sigma_i, sigma_j)^delta\n   \
    \   # Prepare per-edge scaling via broadcasting\n      sigma_i = sigma[:, None]  # (n, kq)\n      sigma_j = sigma[idx]      # (n, kq)\n      sigma_ij = 0.5 * (sigma_i + sigma_j)\n      # Avoid divide by zero\n      scaled = d / (np.power(sigma_ij, delta) + 1e-12)\n\n      # Base weights from scaled distances (monotone decreasing)\n      eps = 1e-12\n      w = 1.0 / (scaled + eps)\n\n      # Keep top-k by weight per row to form sparse graph\n      k_keep = int(min(k, w.shape[1]))\n      # Argpartition per row\n      part = np.argpartition(-w, kth=k_keep - 1, axis=1)[:, :k_keep]\n      # Gather top-k neighbors and weights\n      row_ids = np.arange(n)[:, None]\n      top_idx = idx[row_ids, part]\n      top_w = w[row_ids, part]\n      top_d = scaled[row_ids, part]\n\n      # Build directional sparse matrices\n      rows = row_ids.repeat(k_keep, axis=1).ravel()\n      cols = top_idx.ravel()\n      data_w = top_w.ravel()\n      data_d = top_d.ravel()\n\n      W_dir = sp.coo_matrix((data_w,\
    \ (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      D_dir = sp.coo_matrix((data_d, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n\n      # Symmetrize: union with max for weights/distances\n      W_sym = W_dir.maximum(W_dir.T)\n      D_sym = D_dir.minimum(D_dir.T)  # for distances, use min across directions\n\n      # Soft reciprocal boost: identify mutual edges (present both ways)\n      # Approximate mutual via intersection > 0 in both directions\n      mutual_mask = W_dir.minimum(W_dir.T)\n      # mutual edges weights approximate by min directional weight\n      W_mutual = mutual_mask\n      # Scale mutual edges by a small factor to reward stable links\n      mu = 1.10\n      # Combine: (mu * mutual) + (remaining union - mutual)\n      W_final = W_sym + (mu - 1.0) * W_mutual\n\n      # Ensure strictly CSR float64 for metrics\n      W_final = W_final.tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      D_sym = D_sym.tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n\n      # Optional row-normalization of connectivities to [0,1] scale per\
    \ node\n      row_sum = np.asarray(W_final.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n      row_sum[row_sum == 0] = 1.0\n      W_final = _row_scale_csr(W_final, 1.0 / row_sum)\n      return W_final, D_sym\n\n  # ---- Submission entrypoint ----\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      MR–SelfTune Graph PCA (ridge hyperparams).\n      - Normalizes raw counts, log1p\n      - HVG selection via var['batch_hvg'] if available\n      - TruncatedSVD to 100 PCs\n      - kNN with self-tuned local scaling (kd=22, δ=0.10)\n      - Weighted connectivities via 1/(eps+scaled_distance) with soft reciprocal boost\n      Returns:\n        adata with obsm['X_emb'], obsp['connectivities'], obsp['distances'].\n      \"\"\"\n      rng = np.random.RandomState(0)\n      # Hyperparameters (Praxis ridge)\n      n_pcs = 100\n      k = 50\n      kd = 22\n      delta = 0.10\n\n      # 1) Normalize from counts and select features\n      Xn = _normalize_log1p_from_counts(adata)\n      Xn\
    \ = _select_features(Xn, adata)\n\n      # 2) PCs\n      Z = _pca_truncated(Xn, n_components=n_pcs, random_state=0)\n\n      # 3) Graph\n      C, D = _build_knn(Z, k=k, kd=kd, delta=delta, extra=10, n_jobs=-1)\n\n      # 4) Write results\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Z.astype(np.float32)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = C\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = D\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning research previews**\n\nI’m thinking we should preview Praxis capsules to get an overview before diving into the full details. My plan is to pin key capsules like \"Final Handover v4.7\" and \"Handover TL;DR.\" Then, I’ll check submissions by Praxis lineage in the Research Counter, but there are so many on the scoreboard! I might also browse the code modules in storage/praxis. We should list the top-level directories to manage the 561 files more easily. I'll ensure we keep track of tokens during this process.**Previewing submissions in Research Counter**\n\nI’m planning to go to the Research Counter to preview the last 100 submissions. I’ll use /execute_action{preview all}, but I need to be mindful of token costs. The preview will display titles, tags, abstracts, and scores for the latest 100, sorted by score. It’s important to check for tags, which aren’t visible on the scoreboard but should show up in the preview. First, I want to read \"Final Handover v4.7\"\
  \ to understand our current baseline and next steps for minimal-delta improvement.**Proposing a minimal-delta experiment**\n\nI need to propose a quick experiment focusing on a minimal-delta idea. I’ll investigate techniques like λ auto scheduling, cross-validated smoothing, or local normalization of graph edge weights using a per-batch kNN denominator. Notably, the interesting metric Graph_conn is hovering between 0.967 and 0.985, with small differences. The state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of 0.7465 has a Graph_conn of 0.968. Improvements seem to be only small increments, making it a great opportunity to explore potential axes for enhancement.**Analyzing top results for improvements**\n\nIt looks like the top results on the scoreboard don't include BRBG+DAQB. The leading method is the MPB‑GraphFWPCA override from Praxis III, which might perform better without BRBG. For instance, the combination \"MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB: kd=24\" only achieved a score of 0.7426, indicating BRBG+DAQB\
  \ may not have been beneficial.\n\nI’m considering implementing “Batch-entropy regularized connectivities” or “quota-preserving kNN graph assembly” for better batch proportion. Another idea includes using feature weighting with batch-aware HVGs to normalize across batches, though that might be a heavier solution. Pre-normalization like sctransform could also be an option.**Exploring integration approaches**\n\nSeems like I'm looking at various integration strategies, but I can't utilize BRBG+DAQB since they haven't produced strong results. I'm considering implementing batch-entropy regularization or using mutual nearest neighbors with density-regularized edges. \n\nAnother thought is to try feature weighting with batch-aware HVGs, but this can be heavy. \n\nI could also use harmonic mean merging for ensemble connectivity or edge debiasing by local batch frequency equalizer to ensure fair representation across batches. However, I'll need to tread carefully to maintain cell type separation.\
  \ \n\nOverall, a minimal-delta proposal to implement \"Mutual Rank kNN Graph for GraphFWPCA\" seems promising, with structured steps to process counts, standardize genes, and compute distances and neighbor rankings efficiently.**Planning graph implementation**\n\nI’m considering the best path to create a valid graph under the conditions. My baseline is targeting a yield of around 0.72-0.74 to match the ridge metrics. It’s essential to check the \"Final Handover v4.7\" for specific ridge hyperparameters first. \n\nI could implement a \"Ridge MR-kNN GraphFWPCA\" using the appropriate kd and δ values. To optimize the calculations for neighbors and distances, I’ll use Scanpy and potentially leverage mutual rank weighting for weighted connectivities. The implementation will involve normalizing raw counts carefully, ensuring no modifications to the original data and creating normalized output layers. Let's preview the required documents to align with our plan before finalizing the code!**Implementing\
  \ adjacency construction**\n\nI’m considering how to create the normalized counts and handle the data effectively. I'll use the raw counts and transform them into log-scaled values through normalization. It’s critical to ensure we restrict our features to highly variable genes (HVGs) and standardize them properly.\n\nNext, I plan to compute the principal components using TruncatedSVD for efficient dimensionality reduction, avoiding excessive memory usage. The local scaling process will involve neighbor distances, using the scikit-learn NearestNeighbors method. Lastly, I’ll construct a symmetric adjacency matrix based on computed weights to finalize the connections. I hope all this runs smoothly within the time limits!**Finalizing implementation details**\n\nI’m ready to finalize my project with the title \"MR-GraphFWPCA (ridge) δ=0.10 kd=22 k=50 seed=0\" and five relevant tags like graph, PCA, and mutual-rank. The abstract will detail the implementation of GraphFWPCA with kNN and density\
  \ scaling.\n\nNext, I'll preview some documents in Private Memory to align, then plan to submit my code using the appropriate imports and structures. I'll ensure to adapt hyperparameters and maintain efficient memory usage in the implementation for processing neighbor ranks and constructing the adjacency matrix. I hope this all runs smoothly!**Implementing reciprocal-only weighting**\n\nI’m thinking of using a simpler approach by implementing \"reciprocal-only (BRBG)\" weights instead of MR weighting. My plan is to set a base weight where W_ij = 1.0 for neighbors, while non-mutual neighbors are reduced by a gamma factor. \n\nTo form the weighted adjacency, I’ll use a symmetry approach and a tie-breaker for reciprocal weighting. I’ll compute connectivities as an adjacency matrix and ensure mutual weighting is appropriately represented. The final steps involve normalizing the result, preparing for the \"Final Handover,\" and crafting my submission code efficiently.**Crafting code step-by-step**\n\
  \nI’m getting ready to write the code piece by piece. Since the \"batch_hvg\" variable might not exist, I’ll use all genes instead. I’ll implement some convenience functions from Scanpy like normalization and PCA.\n\nI need to ensure the code aligns with relevant versions, and I’ll set a random seed for reproducibility. I’ll deliver the final message in a YAML format, keeping in mind the importance of quoting any strings with special characters. Now, I’m preparing to execute the necessary commands to read memory capsules and submit my work."
