parts:
- text: "/execute_action{storage write praxis/fw_pca_batchresid.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.decomposition import TruncatedSVD\n  import anndata as ad\n  import pandas as pd\n\n  # --------- Sparse helpers ---------\n  def _to_csr(X):\n      if sp.isspmatrix_csr(X):\n          return X\n      if sp.issparse(X):\n          return X.tocsr()\n      return sp.csr_matrix(X)\n\n  def _row_scale_csr(X, scale):\n      if not sp.isspmatrix_csr(X):\n          X = _to_csr(X)\n      X = X.copy()\n      X.data *= np.repeat(scale, np.diff(X.indptr))\n      return X\n\n  def _log1p_csr_inplace(X):\n      X = _to_csr(X)\n      X = X.copy()\n      X.data = np.log1p(X.data)\n      return X\n\n  # --------- Preprocessing ---------\n  def _normalize_log1p_from_counts(adata):\n      Xc = adata.layers['counts'] if ('counts' in adata.layers) else adata.X\n      Xc = _to_csr(Xc).astype(np.float32)\n      lib = np.asarray(Xc.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n  \
    \    lib = np.maximum(lib, 1e-9)\n      scale = (1e4 / lib).astype(np.float32)\n      Xn = _row_scale_csr(Xc, scale)\n      Xn = _log1p_csr_inplace(Xn)\n      return Xn\n\n  def _select_features(X, adata):\n      if 'batch_hvg' in adata.var.columns:\n          mask = adata.var['batch_hvg'].values.astype(bool)\n          if mask.sum() > 0 and mask.sum() < X.shape[1]:\n              return X[:, mask], mask\n      return X, None\n\n  # --------- Batch R^2 feature weights ---------\n  def _gene_batch_r2(X, batch_codes):\n      \"\"\"\n      Compute R^2 per gene for regression on batch (intercept + (B-1) dummies).\n      X: CSR (n x g), batch_codes: int array shape (n,)\n      Returns r2: (g,)\n      \"\"\"\n      n, g = X.shape\n      nb = int(batch_codes.max()) + 1\n      onehot = np.eye(nb, dtype=np.float32)[batch_codes]  # (n, nb)\n      # Drop last column to avoid intercept collinearity\n      D = np.concatenate([np.ones((n, 1), dtype=np.float32), onehot[:, :-1]], axis=1)  # (n, p)\n\
    \      # Precompute\n      G = D.T @ D  # (p, p)\n      Ginv = np.linalg.pinv(G)  # robust to singularities\n      # T = X^T D  -> shape (g, p)\n      T = X.T @ D  # works with CSR @ dense -> dense (g, p)\n      # yTy and y_sum for SST\n      yTy = np.asarray(X.power(2).sum(axis=0)).ravel()  # (g,)\n      y_sum = np.asarray(X.sum(axis=0)).ravel()         # (g,)\n      sst = yTy - (y_sum ** 2) / float(n)\n      # quad form per gene: t^T Ginv t\n      # Use einsum for efficiency: (g,p),(p,p),(g,p) -> (g,)\n      quad = np.einsum('gp,pq,gq->g', T, Ginv, T, optimize=True)\n      sse = np.maximum(yTy - quad, 0.0)  # SSE without explicit mean-centering in D (intercept handles mean)\n      # Guard against zero variance genes\n      sst = np.maximum(sst, 1e-12)\n      r2 = 1.0 - (sse / sst)\n      r2 = np.clip(r2, 0.0, 1.0)\n      return r2\n\n  def _apply_feature_weights(X, w):\n      \"\"\"\n      Column-scale X by sqrt(w). X is CSR (n x g), w is (g,)\n      \"\"\"\n      W = sp.diags(np.sqrt(w).astype(np.float32))\n\
    \      return X @ W\n\n  # --------- PCA ---------\n  def _pca_sparse(X, n_components=100, random_state=0):\n      n_cells, n_genes = X.shape\n      n_components = int(min(n_components, max(2, min(n_cells - 1, n_genes))))\n      svd = TruncatedSVD(n_components=n_components, random_state=random_state)\n      Z = svd.fit_transform(X)\n      return Z.astype(np.float32)\n\n  def _batch_center_rows(Z, batch_codes):\n      Zc = Z.copy()\n      for b in np.unique(batch_codes):\n          m = (batch_codes == b)\n          if m.any():\n              Zc[m] -= Zc[m].mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n      return Zc\n\n  # --------- Entry Point ---------\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      BR-WPCA (Batch-Residual Weighted PCA) with scanpy neighbors:\n      - Normalize raw counts (1e4) + log1p\n      - Use batch-aware HVGs if available\n      - Compute per-gene R^2 (batch-only linear model); weight genes by (1 - R^2)^beta\n      - PCA to 100 dims\n \
    \     - Per-batch mean-centering in PC space\n      Return embedding only (let evaluator build neighbors), to improve LISI compatibility.\n      \"\"\"\n      if 'batch' not in adata.obs.columns:\n          raise ValueError(\"adata.obs['batch'] not found.\")\n      batch_cat = adata.obs['batch']\n      if not pd.api.types.is_categorical_dtype(batch_cat):\n          batch_cat = batch_cat.astype('category')\n      batch_codes = batch_cat.cat.codes.values.astype(int)\n\n      # Hyperparameters\n      n_pcs = 100\n      beta = 0.5     # soften weights\n      w_min = 0.10   # floor to avoid collapsing features\n\n      # 1) Normalize + feature subset\n      Xn = _normalize_log1p_from_counts(adata)\n      Xn, mask = _select_features(Xn, adata)\n\n      # 2) Feature weights from batch R^2\n      r2 = _gene_batch_r2(Xn, batch_codes)\n      w = np.power(1.0 - r2, beta)\n      w = np.clip(w, w_min, 1.0)\n\n      # 3) Apply feature weights and PCA\n      Xw = _apply_feature_weights(Xn, w)\n   \
    \   Z = _pca_sparse(Xw, n_components=n_pcs, random_state=0)\n\n      # 4) Batch-center PCs (means only)\n      Zc = _batch_center_rows(Z, batch_codes)\n\n      # 5) Output embedding only\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zc.astype(np.float32)\n      return adata\n```\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BR‑WPCA (Batch‑Residual Weighted PCA) + batch-centering, nPC=100 (embed-only)\"\ntags: \"feature-weighting, pca, batch-regression, embed-only, sparse\"\nabstract: \"Compute gene weights from batch R^2 (intercept+dummies), downweight batch-driven genes w=(1−R2)^0.5, PCA to 100 dims, and per-batch mean-center PCs. Return only X_emb so evaluator builds neighbors. Hypothesis: boosts iLISI/kBET and PCR without harming cLISI/Graph_conn.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from fw_pca_batchresid import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as impl\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata):\n      return impl(adata)\n```\n/execute_action{review 1366}\n/execute_action{rank\
    \ author}"
thinking_content: "**Analyzing LISI and kBET values**\n\nI noticed that \"kBET\" is 0.553, which is considered moderate, but the iLISI value is extremely low. I’m wondering why there's such a discrepancy. LISI uses 90 neighbors, while our graph only has 50, which might lead to infinite distances for edges not included in the graph. If the graph isn't large enough, LISI could produce low values due to insufficient neighbor edges. It appears earlier methods with k=50 and relevant distances were more effective, while LISI's use of 90 neighbors potentially complicates things.**Examining LISI and neighbors**\n\nI'm trying to understand the processing function more clearly. If 'X_emb' is present but 'neighbors' isn’t in the uns object, then sc.pp.neighbors is called with a default of n_neighbors set to 15. If connectivities are provided, LISI uses those instead. It seems many previous methods achieved better results by providing connectivities with k set to 90, while some earlier top results used\
  \ k=50 and still got high iLISI values. It raises the question of why our iLISI is near zero given our preparations.**Analyzing graph integration results**\n\nI’m trying to pinpoint what’s going wrong. We set obsm['X_emb'] to Z floats, but it seems we didn’t provide the uns neighbors mapping for 'connectivities' and 'distances'. In the process_integration_output, if both exist, they should add that mapping. LISI uses these to find graph keys, which sounds good. However, our simple Graph PCA scores show ARI at 0.720 and NMI at 0.717, which are similar to ridge SOTA, but the low iLISI value raises concerns about graph computation or densification issues.**Exploring potential clustering issues**\n\nI’ve spotted a potential bug: if embedding exists but there are no neighbors, it raises the question of whether we compute kNN from the embedding. However, when connectivities are provided, clustering may not be performed as expected. Despite this, clustering results still output via leiden using\
  \ our connectivities, which is a good sign. If our graph is overly sparse, LISI could be low. There’s an alternative plan to use scanpy neighbors with just the embedded data, which could improve LISI scores. But previous results show a common trend of low iLISI for graphs like ours, even with better methods indicating effective mixing.**Evaluating iLISI improvement strategies**\n\nOur iLISI is only 0.096, which is far below the desired threshold of 0.95, indicating that our method may not be effective. To improve this, we need to incorporate GraphFWPCA-style weighting. Currently, we aren't yet employing feature-weighted Graph PCA, but I think we could replicate a minimal baseline using weighted PCA based on adjacency. The low iLISI could stem from the graph being too confined to within-batch connections. To enhance the iLISI score, we might consider adjusting for batch effects in our embedding representation, aiming for improved distances that better reflect corrected representations.**Analyzing\
  \ PCR and iLISI results**\n\nUpon reviewing our first PCR in the simple Graph PCA, it was 0.0, indicating that raw distance embeddings didn’t remove batch effects effectively. After batch centering, we achieved 1.0, but the iLISI score remains low at 0.02. This suggests our neighbors may not be capturing sufficient mixing due to strong batch-specific clustering. I propose implementing a GraphFWPCA method by incorporating feature-weighted PCA and calculating per-gene batch variance ratios to down-weight batch-specific genes to improve results. We must also ensure that the distances reflect meaningful relationships in the embeddings while avoiding potential biases related to batch effects.**Computing R² for gene weights**\n\nI need to compute \\( y_{bar} \\) per gene effectively, then calculate the SSE (Sum of Squared Errors) using the formula \\( SSE = \\sum{(y - \\hat{y})^2} \\). Instead of using large matrices, I'll work with normal equations and smaller matrices. The approach to compute\
  \ the R² per gene allows me to use matrix multiplication efficiently while handling sparse matrices. This will enable me to calculate gene weights based on R², ensuring effective scaling, and I’ll implement all this while keeping memory use manageable. With various adjustments, I aim to improve the iLISI score effectively.**Implementing batch-residual PCA**\n\nI need to consider using Euclidean distances since our earlier work suggested that. The iLISI metric is low, indicating mixed contributions may not be sufficient. I plan to use the Scanpy neighbors function without providing connectivities, relying instead on the embedding. My strategy involves normalizing counts, calculating gene weights based on batch R², and conducting a weighted PCA for embedding.\n\nThe designed pipeline will include efficient calculations using matrix multiplications and aim to ensure stability in weights. I want to implement all components correctly while focusing on improving the overall results!**Finalizing\
  \ batch R² computation**\n\nI’m working on edge cases regarding the computation of R² for gene features using high-variance genes. When calculating, I need to ensure I'm focusing on only the appropriate features from Xn and masking out batches as necessary. \n\nAdditionally, I’ll use the Scanpy neighbors function with embedded X to optimize neighbor calculations automatically. For the implementation, I’ll create a vectorized function to compute the batch R², ensuring to build the design matrix correctly while avoiding multicollinearity. I’ll use one-hot encoding through pandas and carefully concatenate matrices to finalize this efficiently!**Preparing for submission**\n\nI'm getting ready to submit my commands and wrap the implementation call. I'll generate a command to write the code for the PCA batch residuals and then handle the submission and author ranking. \n\nAdditionally, I’ll check the review status to ensure everything is ready. I’m considering the memory dimensions, like calculating\
  \ Xn.T @ D, which will yield a manageable size. I’ll make sure to import necessary libraries like NumPy and keep the code clean and minimal while focusing on the essential components for efficiency. Let’s finalize this!"
