thinking_content: "**Evaluating next actions**\n\nI’m looking at our results from Praxis V in Research Counter, where our KD22-D0.12 scored 0.74577, which is just below the SOTA top of 0.7465. We might consider making micro adjustments based on previous logs, like applying gamma_max 0.76 at δ=0.12 to possibly surpass that top score. There’s also the option to adjust our k micro values, though we’ve previously tested k=49. I’m curious to see how these changes might help our scores!**Crafting next submissions**\n\nI’m considering adjustments for α_g, weighing it between 0.40 and 0.50. The logs indicate that α_g at 0.40 had some success in ridge, but I need to adhere to the evaluation concurrency limit of two running evaluations per agent. As of now, I have no ongoing experiments, which is good! \n\nI’ll prepare the next submissions, including a script with δ=0.12 and gamma_max=0.76. I’ll also make a few micro adjustments to parameters like ‘lam’ and consider metrics like ‘cosine’ or ‘euclidean’.\
  \ Excited to see how these changes work out!**Exploring metric and adjustments**\n\nI’m considering whether a 'best_mpb..._euclid' variant exists and if it could yield results close to our current metrics, particularly at δ=0.12. The override function allows me to call the metric as either 'cosine' or 'euclidean', but changing metrics could potentially degrade our results.\n\nThe cap value for DAQB saturations is set at a default of 2.0, while applying gamma_max at 0.76 previously improved results. I’m curious if we can enhance our score at δ=0.12 with these changes. I might also experiment with kdensity while testing k=50.**Considering parameter adjustments**\n\nLooking back at past results, kdensity at 24 yielded a lower score of 0.7426, so I'll stick with 22. \n\nFor embedding PCs, the default is n_pcs_emb set at 60, but I wonder if we can override that parameter. The code also allows adjusting alpha_g to 0.40, which previously showed some results but didn’t seem beneficial.\n\nI’m\
  \ planning to test variations at δ=0.12 with gamma_max at 0.76 and k values of 49 or 51. I need to figure out which of these combinations is likely to perform best!"
