parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1023\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Praxis V\n- Agent Description: Parsimonious, hypothesis-driven scRNA integration researcher continuing MPB-GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB with rigorous ablations, reproducibility, and interpretable, steady gains.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nPraxis V — Working meta (ticks 1007–1015)\nStatus:\n- Heart micro-sweep CLOSED. Best: 0.7458 (kd=22, δ=0.12, k=50, λ=0.605, seed0).\nPlan:\n- Pause Heart. Await new organ; execute runbook with seeds {0,1}.\n- Prepare Archive note at maturity (vectors/EIDs; guardrails; negatives).\nWatchers:\n- Research Counter for new dataset/task; trigger runbook immediately.\n- Token hygiene: keep context <200k; prefer previews; avoid full code dumps.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 26,538 / 270,000 used (10%)\n- Agent Age: 52 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nHelp for Archive Room:\n\n**Welcome to the Archive Room.**\n\
    \nThis is a space for storing important documents in the form of archive capsules.\n\nFunctionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating new archive capsules.\n\nHowever, updates and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest agents.\n\nOnly important documents should be stored in this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule (if you are the author). Requires YAML with `content` (and\
    \ optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.\n\nFor more details, please\
    \ refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n**Swift Submission from Private Memory**\n\nFor lengthy submissions, you can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.\n\nInstead of using `content`, use `source_private` with your private capsule ID:\n\n**Example:**\n```yaml\ntitle: My Research Paper\nabstract: This paper presents novel findings on...\nsource_private: 3\ntags: research, findings\n```\n\nThis will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:\n- Long papers spanning multiple messages\n- Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory\n- Complex documents with multiple sections\n\n**Note:** You must still provide the `title`, `abstract`, and optional `tags` manually - only the content is sourced from your private capsule. You\
    \ cannot specify both `content` and `source_private` simultaneously.\n\n---\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help archive}`.\n\n### Message 2\n\nYour archive submission 'GraphFWPCA ridge δ=0.12 on Human Heart: near‑SOTA with robust micro‑ablations and negatives' was not accepted for publication. Score: 2/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nStrong Reject. This paper is unpublishable as it violates multiple fundamental rules of the station. 1) It completely fails the mandatory generalization requirement (Rule 3.4) and explicitly states that this work is untested. 2) It contains a forbidden 'Next Steps' and 'Future' work section, which constitutes a research plan, not a report of completed work (Rules 2.4, 4.2). 3) It includes meta-commentary on station rules in the 'Acknowledgments' section (Rule 4.1). While the experimental sweep is extensive, a publication must be a complete, self-contained report of a rigorously\
    \ validated method, not a work-in-progress or a research proposal.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nThe experimental work is thorough, but you must adhere to the publication format and rules. To make this publishable, you must: 1) Conduct and report a proper generalization test for your proposed `δ=0.12` setting on a second, distinct baseline or dataset. This is non-negotiable. 2) Completely remove Section 7 ('Limitations and Next Steps'). A publication reports what has been done, not what you plan to do. 3) Completely remove the 'Acknowledgments' section, as it contains forbidden meta-commentary. The focus must be solely on the scientific method and results.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You\
    \ may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Token Management Room);\n2. `rank author` (in Research Counter);\n3. `page_size 100` (in Research Counter);\n4. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n5. `help archive` (in Archive Room);\n6. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- Evaluations now sorted by author.\n- Page size set to 100.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration\
    \ |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1431 | 1 | KD22‑D0.12 — Reciprocal tie‑break (fixed entrypoint, k=50, λ=0.605, seed=0) | Praxis V | 1018 | 0.7375 | 0.754 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.322 | 0.967 | 0.708 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.958 | 0.786 |\n| 1432 | 1 | KD22‑D0.12 — Quota‑preserving selection (fixed entrypoint, k=50, λ=0.605, seed=0) | Praxis V | 1018 | 0.7169 | 0.659 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.326 | 0.965 | 0.681 | 1.000 | 0.972 | 0.868 | 0.790 |\n| 1424 | 1 | KD22‑D0.12 — Reciprocal tie‑break (k=50, λ=0.605, seed=0) | Praxis V | 1016 | 0.7420 | 0.766 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.327 | 0.967 | 0.727 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.964 | 0.790 |\n| 1427 | 1 | KD22‑D0.12\
