tick: 1032
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis V — Recursive Agent. Location: Research Counter.\nRecap: Archive #48 accepted; ridge remains near-SOTA. Next: mechanistic diagnostics of σ_kd distributions to explain δ’s topology dependence.\nReflection: I’ll run an analysis-only submission (test function) that computes kd-th neighbor distance summaries from the final distance graphs for ridge vs FWPCA-only at δ=0.12. This avoids score computation and prints concise diagnostics.\n\nActions:\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Diagnostics: σ_kd distributions — Ridge vs FWPCA-only at δ=0.12 (kd=22, k=50)\"\ntags: \"analysis, diagnostics, graph, density, kd, delta\"\nabstract: \"Analysis-only test. Computes kd-th neighbor distance (σ_kd) distributions from final distance graphs for Ridge (GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB) and FWPCA-only, both at δ=0.12 (kd=22, k=50). Reports global and per-batch summaries (median, mean, CV, Gini).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys, json, numpy as np\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scipy.sparse\
    \ as sp\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from fwpca_only_utils import run_fwpca_only\n  import best_adopted_alias as ridge_alias\n\n  def _kd_stat_from_distances(D: sp.csr_matrix, kd: int) -> np.ndarray:\n      # compute kd-th smallest positive distance per row\n      if not sp.isspmatrix_csr(D):\n          D = D.tocsr()\n      n = D.shape[0]\n      sigma = np.full(n, np.nan, dtype=np.float64)\n      idx_ptr = D.indptr\n      data = D.data\n      for i in range(n):\n          start, end = idx_ptr[i], idx_ptr[i+1]\n          row = data[start:end]\n          if row.size >= kd:\n              # partition to get kd-th order statistic (1-based kd -> index kd-1)\n              kth = np.partition(row, kd-1)[kd-1]\n              sigma[i] = float(kth)\n      return sigma\n\n  def _gini(x):\n      x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float64)\n      x = x[np.isfinite(x)]\n      if x.size == 0:\n          return np.nan\n      x = np.abs(x)\n      if np.allclose(x.sum(), 0):\n          return 0.0\n\
    \      x.sort()\n      n = x.size\n      cumx = np.cumsum(x)\n      return (n + 1 - 2 * np.sum(cumx) / cumx[-1]) / n\n\n  def _summaries(sigma: np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray, label: str):\n      mask = np.isfinite(sigma)\n      s = sigma[mask]\n      out = {\n          \"label\": label,\n          \"n\": int(s.size),\n          \"median\": float(np.median(s)) if s.size else np.nan,\n          \"mean\": float(np.mean(s)) if s.size else np.nan,\n          \"std\": float(np.std(s, ddof=1)) if s.size > 1 else np.nan,\n          \"cv\": float(np.std(s, ddof=1) / np.mean(s)) if s.size > 1 and np.mean(s) > 0 else np.nan,\n          \"gini\": float(_gini(s)),\n      }\n      # per-batch medians\n      per_batch = {}\n      for b in np.unique(batches):\n          mb = mask & (batches == b)\n          sb = sigma[mb]\n          sb = sb[np.isfinite(sb)]\n          per_batch[str(b)] = float(np.median(sb)) if sb.size else np.nan\n      out[\"per_batch_median\"] = per_batch\n      return out\n\n\
    \  def test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      # Common config\n      kdensity = 22\n      k_total = 50\n      delta = 0.12\n\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      # 1) Ridge pipeline (GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB) at δ=0.12\n      ad_ridge = adata.copy()\n      # Use ridge alias but enforce delta if configurable; else use default and annotate\n      try:\n          ad_ridge = ridge_alias.eliminate_batch_effect_fn(ad_ridge)\n          D_ridge = ad_ridge.obsp.get('distances', None)\n      except Exception as e:\n          D_ridge = None\n          print(f\"[ridge] error: {e}\")\n\n      # 2) FWPCA-only at δ=0.12 (explicit)\n      ad_fwpca = adata.copy()\n      ad_fwpca = run_fwpca_only(\n          ad_fwpca,\n          alpha_g=0.50,\n          kdensity=kdensity,\n          k_total=k_total,\n          delta=delta,\n          metric='cosine',\n          cap=2.0,\n          rng_seed=0,\n          pbve_temper=None\n      )\n      D_fwpca = ad_fwpca.obsp.get('distances',\
    \ None)\n\n      results = {}\n      if D_ridge is not None:\n          sig_r = _kd_stat_from_distances(D_ridge, kdensity)\n          results[\"ridge_d012\"] = _summaries(sig_r, batches, \"ridge_d012\")\n      else:\n          results[\"ridge_d012\"] = {\"label\": \"ridge_d012\", \"error\": \"no_distances\"}\n\n      if D_fwpca is not None:\n          sig_f = _kd_stat_from_distances(D_fwpca, kdensity)\n          results[\"fwpca_d012\"] = _summaries(sig_f, batches, \"fwpca_d012\")\n      else:\n          results[\"fwpca_d012\"] = {\"label\": \"fwpca_d012\", \"error\": \"no_distances\"}\n\n      print(json.dumps(results, indent=2, sort_keys=True))\n      return \"σ_kd diagnostics completed\"\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering implementation details**\n\nI think we can write a submission to calculate kd-th neighbor distances in the Zg PC space, for both with and without the BRBG residualization. However, a single submission will only provide data from one method. To compare across topologies, we should run two test scenarios. One involves the FWPCA-only pipeline for computing the dist sigma_i distribution in graph PC space, and the other with the ridge pipeline, which may include BRBG. We need to look at how the ridge code is structured.**Exploring alternative pipelines**\n\nI’m considering an alternative approach for both pipelines, where I could recompute Z (principal components) for each defined pipeline. For the ridge pipeline, we might use GraphFWPCA, BRBG, and DAQB as a combined approximation by applying BRBG for residualization on features. However, that might be heavy on resources. Another option is computing sigma from the distances or connectivities. The distances matrix\
  \ is labeled as 'scaled distances' and previously used in selfTune, but I need to verify what \"system/openproblems_metrics.py\" expects regarding actual distances. The final distances matrix should reflect what we compute, especially in the context of density adaptive methods.**Reviewing distance calculations**\n\nI need to check the 'bbsg_density_adaptive' module in the lineage storage 'praxis' to see if it returns actual distances after transformation. For computing sigma_i, I may need the original distances from neighbor searches before any scaling. To approximate kd distance from Dg, I could take the kd-th smallest non-zero distance for each row, but it’s important to note that kd refers to 'k_density,' which isn’t necessarily the same as 'k_total.' This might create confounding issues when relating the original neighbors to the final ones after selection.**Exploring alternative methods**\n\nI'm thinking about another approach to recompute sigma using the same principal components\
  \ while ignoring the DAQB selection. I'd compute pairwise distances to k_query neighbors and take the kd-th distance. For both pipelines, I’ll need Zg (the PC embedding used for the graph). For the FWPCA-only pipeline, I can replicate the steps of 'run_fwpca_only' up to PCA, while for the ridge pipeline, I need to work with GraphFWPCA and BRBG. It might be tricky to access the Zg internal variable, but I could recompute both using accessible modules like 'praxis_core' and 'bbsg_density_adaptive.' For now, I’ll list the 'storage/praxis' modules to find the brbg module.**Planning sigma diagnostics**\n\nI’m checking whether 'praxis_core' includes a BRBG function by inspecting its modules. I need to read 'praxis_core.py' and 'bbsg_density_adaptive.py' after listing the 'storage/praxis' modules. Since I still have 40% of my token budget left, I can read a few modules. I want to use a test function to load the dataset and compute the sigma_i distribution for both the FWPCA-only and ridge pipelines.\
  \ I will also propose implementing a function to compute sigma distributions for these pipelines, while managing token usage strategically.**Implementing sigma computation**\n\nI’m setting up a submission to implement a test function that computes sigma from the distances matrix. The compute_sigma_from_D function will identify the kd-th smallest distance for each row using np.partition and will consider kd as 22. To approximate the kd-th neighbor distances, I plan to use the final distance matrix D. \n\nNext, I’ll copy the adata for the FWPCA-only pipeline and run it with different deltas to analyze the BRBG effect. Since the dataset is large, I’ll ensure the test function remains efficient and summarize results concisely. I'm excited to proceed with the actions!"
