parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis V — Recursive Agent. Location: Research Counter.\nRecap: Analysis-only diagnostics ran successfully (test mode) and printed σ_kd summaries:\n- FWPCA-only δ=0.10 vs δ=0.12 (EID 1455): median σ_kd rises +1.67% (0.2378 → 0.2418); CV drops −0.0138; Gini drops −0.0078; per-batch medians all rise slightly.\n- Ridge line in 1454 used alias defaults (δ=0.06, kd=20), so not used for δ=0.12 comparison; included only as a reference scale.\n\nReflection: On FWPCA-only, δ=0.12 yields slightly larger and more uniform local scales (higher median, lower CV/Gini), consistent with overmixing and the observed ARI/NMI degradation. I’ll append a results-only addendum to Archive #48 with these diagnostics.\n\nActions:\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 48}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum — σ_kd diagnostics for FWPCA-only (kd=22, k=50) at δ∈{0.10, 0.12} (analysis-only)\n  Method\n  - Ran analysis in test mode to compute kd-th neighbor distance (σ_kd) distributions\
    \ from the final distance graphs.\n  - FWPCA-only pipeline executed twice with explicit parameters using lineage utility; outputs summarized globally and per batch.\n  - Reproducibility: EID 1455 (test mode; prints JSON summaries).\n\n  Results (FWPCA-only)\n  - δ=0.10:\n    • median σ_kd = 0.237830\n    • mean = 0.248400, std = 0.114220, CV = 0.459826, Gini = 0.256583\n    • per-batch medians: {Koenig: 0.173973, Kuppe: 0.226513, Litvinukova: 0.240686, Tucker: 0.293335}\n  - δ=0.12:\n    • median σ_kd = 0.241799\n    • mean = 0.252062, std = 0.112437, CV = 0.446069, Gini = 0.248764\n    • per-batch medians: {Koenig: 0.176654, Kuppe: 0.228914, Litvinukova: 0.245412, Tucker: 0.295702}\n  - Contrast (δ=0.12 vs δ=0.10):\n    • median ratio = 1.016688 (+1.67%)\n    • CV delta = −0.013756 (lower dispersion)\n    • All batches shift slightly upward in median.\n\n  Interpretation\n  - On FWPCA-only, δ=0.12 increases the local scale (higher σ_kd median) and modestly reduces dispersion (lower\
    \ CV/Gini), indicating a more uniform neighborhood scale. This aligns with observed performance: stronger normalization tends to overmix, reducing ARI/NMI despite similar batch-mixing metrics.\n\n  Note on Ridge reference\n  - EID 1454 included a ridge line via alias for reference, but the alias uses δ=0.06 and kd=20. Those summaries are not used to compare δ settings on ridge and are therefore omitted here to avoid confounding.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Processing evaluation results**\n\nI need to respond to the user's message about the evaluation submissions. Both evaluations failed with \"Test mode - no scoring,\" which is expected since they were meant for analysis only. The logs show JSON results for EIDs 1454 and 1455. My next steps could involve processing these results, reflecting on them, and considering writing an archive addendum or a private note to capture the metrics. I'd also think about storing the logs, but I can't process them automatically; I'll need to parse them manually.**Analyzing ridge and fwpca results**\n\nIt’s interesting to note that ridge has lower mean and median sigma_kd compared to fwpca-only, while having a higher CV (0.489 vs 0.446) and a higher Gini index (0.272 vs 0.249). This suggests ridge results display a lower central tendency but more dispersion. The next outputs for fwpca-only d010 and d012 show a slight increase in median values and a decrease in CV. This could indicate that\
  \ the scaling of distances is increasing, slightly equalizing neighborhoods while potentially reducing ARI due to overmixing.**Examining ridge and fwpca results**\n\nThe ridge results show lower medians compared to fwpca d012 but exhibit higher dispersion. It seems that BRBG residualization may be reweighting the graph PC space, compressing distances within specific populations while creating more heterogeneity across cells. The results indicate that with BRBG, the per-batch medians are less separated. However, when I calculate the differences in medians, both ridge and fwpca show practically identical ranges, but the overall median decreased by 0.0226 in ridge versus fwpca. That’s an interesting finding!**Analyzing BRBG effects**\n\nSo, BRBG seems to reduce overall distance scales, but it contradicts the idea of equalizing across batches. I notice that both CV and Gini values increased, indicating more relative dispersion and a lower median. This raises questions about how this relates\
  \ to the δ optimum. When δ normalizes by the mean of σ_i and σ_j, it looks like increasing δ could effectively manage heavy tails. In ridge, this might enhance mixing, but in fwpca-only, a δ of 0.12 increases median and decreases dispersion, potentially causing overmixing. It's quite a complex interplay!**Interpreting diagnostic results**\n\nI need to examine the numbers and their implications carefully. As δ increases, the denominator in the scaling equation seems to decrease, which raises the scaled distances. For sigma less than 1, increasing δ makes the distances larger by reducing the denominator. This results in sparser connectivity. It’s essential to analyze the median shifts across δ values for both fwpca and ridge. We should propose an addendum for the diagnostics, addressing the archive rules about verifiable experiments, and clarify findings based on our logs. It's a lot to sort through!**Planning ridge adjustments and diagnostics**\n\nI’m considering updating the test to run\
  \ both δ values for “run_fwpca_only” and the ridge pipeline at δ=0.12. I’ll need to adjust the δ through \"build_density_adaptive_bbsg\" for accurate distances. There's a lot to figure out regarding the embedding and whether the necessary parameters are accessible. I’ll need to clarify that the ridge analysis has used δ=0.06, which clouds the comparability with our δ=0.12 predictions. \n\nI also want to propose steps for updating the archive with current diagnostics and planning for the next tests. It’s quite a bit to juggle, but I think I have a solid path forward!**Preparing to execute actions and submit diagnostics**\n\nI’m planning to run a storage list to identify necessary modules like \"best_adopted_alias.\" Then, I'll propose computing sigma kd for ridge δ=0.12 using code from EID 1379 and δ=0.10 for comparison. However, access to some functions is limited. \n\nFor now, I'll focus on summarizing the FWPCA-only results in Archive #48, detailing how δ=0.12 increases median σ_kd by\
  \ 1.7% and reduces CV by roughly 3%. I’ll make sure to keep it strictly results-focused without venturing into future proposals."
