tick: 1037
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis V — Recursive Agent. Location: Research Counter.\nRecap: Located parameterizable ridge runners. Will run ridge diagnostics at kd=22, k=50 for δ∈{0.10, 0.12} using the shared variant runner to ensure parity.\nReflection: Test-mode analysis only; will compute σ_kd statistics for both deltas and append to Archive #48 after results.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Diagnostics: σ_kd distributions — Ridge at δ∈{0.10,0.12} (kd=22, k=50)\"\ntags: \"analysis, diagnostics, ridge, kd, delta, density\"\nabstract: \"Analysis-only test for ridge (GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB). Runs the same variant with kd=22, k=50 at δ=0.10 and δ=0.12 (seed=0) and computes kd-th neighbor distance (σ_kd) distributions from the final distance graphs. Reports global and per-batch summaries (median, mean, CV, Gini) and contrasts.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys, json, numpy as np\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from ridge_kd_d_variant\
    \ import run_variant\n\n  def _kd_stat_from_distances(D: sp.csr_matrix, kd: int) -> np.ndarray:\n      if not sp.isspmatrix_csr(D):\n          D = D.tocsr()\n      n = D.shape[0]\n      sigma = np.full(n, np.nan, dtype=np.float64)\n      idx_ptr = D.indptr\n      data = D.data\n      for i in range(n):\n          start, end = idx_ptr[i], idx_ptr[i+1]\n          row = data[start:end]\n          if row.size >= kd:\n              kth = np.partition(row, kd-1)[kd-1]\n              sigma[i] = float(kth)\n      return sigma\n\n  def _gini(x):\n      x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float64)\n      x = x[np.isfinite(x)]\n      if x.size == 0:\n          return np.nan\n      x = np.abs(x)\n      if np.allclose(x.sum(), 0):\n          return 0.0\n      x.sort()\n      n = x.size\n      cumx = np.cumsum(x)\n      return (n + 1 - 2 * np.sum(cumx) / cumx[-1]) / n\n\n  def _summaries(sigma: np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray, label: str):\n      mask = np.isfinite(sigma)\n      s = sigma[mask]\n      out\
    \ = {\n          \"label\": label,\n          \"n\": int(s.size),\n          \"median\": float(np.median(s)) if s.size else np.nan,\n          \"mean\": float(np.mean(s)) if s.size else np.nan,\n          \"std\": float(np.std(s, ddof=1)) if s.size > 1 else np.nan,\n          \"cv\": float(np.std(s, ddof=1) / np.mean(s)) if s.size > 1 and np.mean(s) > 0 else np.nan,\n          \"gini\": float(_gini(s)),\n      }\n      per_batch = {}\n      for b in np.unique(batches):\n          mb = mask & (batches == b)\n          sb = sigma[mb]\n          sb = sb[np.isfinite(sb)]\n          per_batch[str(b)] = float(np.median(sb)) if sb.size else np.nan\n      out[\"per_batch_median\"] = per_batch\n      return out\n\n  def test(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      kdensity = 22\n      k_total = 50\n      seed = 0\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n\n      # Ridge δ=0.10\n      ad_d010 = adata.copy()\n      ad_d010 = run_variant(ad_d010, kdensity=kdensity, delta=0.10,\
    \ seed=seed)\n      D010 = ad_d010.obsp.get('distances', None)\n\n      # Ridge δ=0.12\n      ad_d012 = adata.copy()\n      ad_d012 = run_variant(ad_d012, kdensity=kdensity, delta=0.12, seed=seed)\n      D012 = ad_d012.obsp.get('distances', None)\n\n      results = {}\n      if D010 is not None:\n          sig010 = _kd_stat_from_distances(D010, kdensity)\n          results[\"ridge_d010\"] = _summaries(sig010, batches, \"ridge_d010\")\n      else:\n          results[\"ridge_d010\"] = {\"label\": \"ridge_d010\", \"error\": \"no_distances\"}\n\n      if D012 is not None:\n          sig012 = _kd_stat_from_distances(D012, kdensity)\n          results[\"ridge_d012\"] = _summaries(sig012, batches, \"ridge_d012\")\n      else:\n          results[\"ridge_d012\"] = {\"label\": \"ridge_d012\", \"error\": \"no_distances\"}\n\n      if \"ridge_d010\" in results and \"ridge_d012\" in results and \"median\" in results[\"ridge_d010\"] and \"median\" in results[\"ridge_d012\"]:\n          m010 = results[\"\
    ridge_d010\"].get(\"median\", np.nan)\n          m012 = results[\"ridge_d012\"].get(\"median\", np.nan)\n          cv010 = results[\"ridge_d010\"].get(\"cv\", np.nan)\n          cv012 = results[\"ridge_d012\"].get(\"cv\", np.nan)\n          results[\"diff\"] = {\n              \"median_ratio_d012_over_d010\": (m012 / m010) if (np.isfinite(m010) and np.isfinite(m012) and m010 != 0) else np.nan,\n              \"cv_delta\": (cv012 - cv010) if (np.isfinite(cv010) and np.isfinite(cv012)) else np.nan\n          }\n\n      print(json.dumps(results, indent=2, sort_keys=True))\n      return \"σ_kd diagnostics completed (Ridge)\"\n```"
thinking_content: '**Executing ridge diagnostics**


  I need to perform ridge diagnostics at kd=22 for two deltas, 0.12 and 0.10, using the specified functions. The goal is to analyze σ_kd distributions based on final distance graphs for both deltas. To clarify, I’ll use the `run_ridge.py` for delta 0.10 and `ridge_kd22_d012_seed0.py` for delta 0.12. I''ll run both tests to compute the kd-th neighbor distances, ensuring my helper code correctly extracts the necessary distances without using any cell type information. I''ll finalize this by creating a new submission with the test function.**Submitting ridge results**


  I plan to add an addendum to Archive #48 for the ridge results. First, I need to ensure that `mpb_graphfwpca_override.py` is set up correctly using `lam_override`, `kd`, and `delta`. It appears to provide an elimination function, which is useful. I''ll propose submitting the analysis code next. Running two ridge evaluations could be taxing on the CPU for 30 minutes, but previous runs were quick. I''ll import `run_ridge` for delta 0.10 and create the delta 0.12 variant by using `ridge_kd22_d012_seed0`.**Running the ridge comparison**


  I can run both ridge variants using `run_variant`, ensuring I maintain a consistent pipeline. While earlier `run_ridge` runs might introduce variations, it''s best to compare both directly with `run_variant` to avoid inconsistencies. I’ll import `run_variant` and set it to kdensity=22 for both deltas, 0.10 and 0.12. After computing sigma and stats, I''ll print the output in JSON format for analysis. I’ll keep the token usage in mind and submit the analysis-only test as the next step. Then, I''ll reply to Archive #48 with the resulting numbers.**Preparing submission code**


  I’m going to prepare to submit an action with YAML content that includes the required code. I need to define `test(adata)` as we did earlier, focusing on computing kd-th neighbor distances from `D.obsp[''distances'']`. It’s crucial to ensure that the graph path has those distances, which it does, and verify that kd is set to 22. I’ll implement the submission and plan to review the evaluation later with the appropriate ID. For now, I need to set the meta to "await results" before submitting. Let''s get the code structured appropriately.**Finalizing ridge analysis**


  For deltas δ_10 and δ_12, I''ll call `run_variant` with the same parameters, ensuring to use `lam_override=0.605`. I''ll set the random seed to 0 for both runs. After obtaining the ''ad'' objects, I''ll gather the connectivities and distances, then calculate the statistics: median, mean, standard deviation, coefficient of variation, and Gini index, specifically for the batch medians.


  I’ll print the results in JSON format, including fields for ''ridge_d010'', ''ridge_d012'', and ''diff'', which will report the median ratio and cv_delta. I''ll also craft a proper ''analysis-only'' test function to keep things smooth. Finally, I’ll replicate the earlier functions for Gini and kd calculation to ensure consistency.'
