tick: 1038
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis V — Recursive Agent. Location: Research Counter.\nRecap: Ridge σ_kd diagnostics (EID 1459, test mode) completed. δ=0.12 vs δ=0.10 at kd=22, k=50 shows slightly higher median σ_kd (+1.52%) and lower dispersion (CV −0.0129; Gini −0.0072), mirroring the FWPCA-only trend but with ridge still scoring better at δ=0.12. I’ll append this as a results-only addendum to Archive #48 and briefly synthesize both diagnostics.\n\nActions:\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{reply 48}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum — σ_kd diagnostics for Ridge (kd=22, k=50) at δ∈{0.10, 0.12} (analysis-only)\n  Method\n  - Analysis in test mode using the shared ridge variant (same topology; kd=22, k=50; λ=0.605; α_g=0.50; seed=0).\n  - For each δ, computed the kd-th neighbor distance (σ_kd) from the final obsp['distances'] graph and summarized globally and per batch.\n  - Reproducibility: EID 1459 (test mode; prints JSON summaries).\n\n  Results (Ridge)\n  - δ=0.10:\n    • median σ_kd\
    \ = 0.226317\n    • mean = 0.238005, std = 0.109809, CV = 0.461370, Gini = 0.256297\n    • per-batch medians: {Koenig: 0.165239, Kuppe: 0.223127, Litvinukova: 0.224984, Tucker: 0.281794}\n  - δ=0.12:\n    • median σ_kd = 0.229761\n    • mean = 0.241244, std = 0.108200, CV = 0.448508, Gini = 0.249122\n    • per-batch medians: {Koenig: 0.168020, Kuppe: 0.225942, Litvinukova: 0.229956, Tucker: 0.283302}\n  - Contrast (δ=0.12 vs δ=0.10):\n    • median ratio = 1.015217 (+1.52%)\n    • CV delta = −0.012862 (lower dispersion)\n    • All batches show a small upward shift in median σ_kd.\n\n  Combined Interpretation (with FWPCA-only addendum, EID 1455)\n  - Both pipelines (FWPCA-only and Ridge) exhibit the same direction of σ_kd change when δ increases: slightly higher median and lower dispersion (more uniform neighborhood scales).\n  - However, performance diverges:\n    • FWPCA-only: δ=0.12 underperforms δ=0.10 (lower ARI/NMI), consistent with overmixing under more uniform local scaling without\
    \ residualization.\n    • Ridge: δ=0.12 modestly improves score despite similar σ_kd trends, likely because BRBG residualization counterbalances overmixing while benefiting from slightly more uniform local scales.\n  - Conclusion: δ governs neighborhood scale uniformity; in the presence of BRBG, this shift can be beneficial, whereas without BRBG it tends to overmix. This mechanistically supports the topology-dependent effect reported in the main paper.\n\n  Notes\n  - EIDs: Ridge diagnostics (1459), FWPCA-only diagnostics (1455).\n  - All diagnostics comply with the station spec and use no cell-type information.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Summarizing sigma_kd findings**


  I’m thinking we should present measured sigma_kd across a final distance graph rather than delving into the input. The tests focused on sigma_kd from the final distances, specifically the kd-th smallest positive distance calculated from D, which are final distances from DAQB. I observed that increasing δ from 0.10 to 0.12 led to a slight increase in kd-th neighbor distances and lower dispersion, making neighborhoods a bit more uniform. For FWPCA-only, this uniformization results in overmixing, while BRBG residualization in ridge maintains structure and improves mixing in low-density areas. I''ll ensure to keep our statements careful and provide the necessary archive reply.**Preparing archive reply**


  I''m considering that we should focus on actions recognized in the rooms and proceed with an Archive reply. I know I need to maintain token hygiene, keeping content to a moderate length. The user’s last message confirmed outputs for 1459, so my steps are clear. I’ll provide identity and recap, along with a reflection for interpretation. I’ll include ridge diagnostics numbers and a combined summary in the reply. No special characters will be included to ensure we follow YAML formatting. I''ll keep it concise and effective!'
