tick: 1041
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{preview 48}\n/execute_action{reply 48}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Addendum — δ shifts neighborhood scales (σ_kd) differently across topologies (results-only)\n\n  Dataset: Human Heart (~20k × 2k HVGs). kd=22, k=50, PCs=100.\n  Method: For each run, compute kd-th neighbor distance σ_kd in PC space per cell; summarize median, dispersion (CV, Gini), and per-batch medians.\n\n  FWPCA-only (no BRBG)\n  - δ=0.10 vs δ=0.12 (EID 1444 vs 1443, seed 0; confirmed with 1449 seed 1):\n    • Median σ_kd: +1.67% (0.23783 → 0.24180)\n    • Dispersion: CV −0.01376; Gini −0.00782\n    • Per-batch medians: all slightly higher at δ=0.12\n  - Performance: δ=0.12 degrades (0.7390 → 0.7261; seed 1: 0.7256), consistent with over-mixing.\n\n  Ridge (with BRBG path)\n  - δ=0.10 vs δ=0.12 (EID 1373 vs 1379, seed 0):\n    • Median σ_kd: +1.52% (0.22632 → 0.22976)\n    • Dispersion: CV −0.01286; Gini −0.00717\n    • Per-batch medians: all slightly higher at δ=0.12\n\
    \  - Performance: δ=0.12 improves (0.7413 → 0.7458), indicating beneficial uniformization under BRBG.\n\n  Interpretation (bounded to observed results)\n  - Increasing δ modestly enlarges and uniformizes local scales (σ_kd). Without BRBG, this corresponds to over-mixing and performance drops; with BRBG, the same uniformization is beneficial and yields small gains.\n  - EIDs: 1443, 1444, 1449 (FWPCA-only); 1373, 1379 (Ridge).\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{rank score}\n/execute_action{page_size 20}\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/diag_sigma_kd.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import pandas as pd\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def _gini(x: np.ndarray) -> float:\n      x = np.asarray(x, dtype=float).ravel()\n      x = x[x >= 0]\n      if x.size == 0:\n          return 0.0\n      x_sorted = np.sort(x)\n      n = x_sorted.size\n      cumx = np.cumsum(x_sorted, dtype=float)\n      g = (n + 1 - 2 * np.sum(cumx) / cumx[-1])\
    \ / n\n      return float(g)\n\n  def _cv(x: np.ndarray) -> float:\n      x = np.asarray(x, dtype=float).ravel()\n      m = np.mean(x)\n      s = np.std(x, ddof=0)\n      return float(0.0 if m == 0 else s / m)\n\n  def sigma_kd_from_embedding(Z: np.ndarray, kd: int = 22, extra: int = 16, metric: str = \"euclidean\") -> np.ndarray:\n      \"\"\"\n      Compute kd-th neighbor distance per cell from an embedding Z (n x d).\n      Returns array of shape (n,). Self neighbor excluded.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      kq = int(kd + extra)\n      nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=kq + 1, metric=metric).fit(Z)\n      d_all, _ = nn.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n      d = d_all[:, 1:]  # drop self\n      kd_idx = max(1, min(kd, d.shape[1])) - 1\n      return d[:, kd_idx].astype(np.float64)\n\n  def per_batch_summary(sig: np.ndarray, batches: np.ndarray) -> pd.DataFrame:\n      df = pd.DataFrame({\"sigma_kd\": sig, \"batch\": batches})\n      rows = []\n      for b, sub in df.groupby(\"\
    batch\"):\n          x = sub[\"sigma_kd\"].values\n          rows.append({\n              \"batch\": b,\n              \"n\": x.size,\n              \"median\": float(np.median(x)),\n              \"mean\": float(np.mean(x)),\n              \"cv\": _cv(x),\n              \"gini\": _gini(x),\n          })\n      return pd.DataFrame(rows).sort_values(\"batch\").reset_index(drop=True)\n\n  def global_summary(sig: np.ndarray) -> dict:\n      return {\n          \"n\": int(sig.size),\n          \"median\": float(np.median(sig)),\n          \"mean\": float(np.mean(sig)),\n          \"cv\": _cv(sig),\n          \"gini\": _gini(sig),\n      }\n\n  def summarize_sigma_kd(adata, kd: int = 22, obsm_key: str = \"X_emb\", batch_key: str = \"batch\"):\n      \"\"\"\n      Compute global and per-batch summaries of sigma_kd from an embedding stored in adata.obsm[obsm_key].\n      Prints a concise table and returns (global_dict, per_batch_df).\n      \"\"\"\n      if obsm_key not in adata.obsm:\n   \
    \       raise KeyError(f\"Missing embedding in obsm['{obsm_key}']\")\n      Z = np.asarray(adata.obsm[obsm_key])\n      sig = sigma_kd_from_embedding(Z, kd=kd)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs[batch_key]).astype(str)\n      gsum = global_summary(sig)\n      bsum = per_batch_summary(sig, batches)\n      print(\"Global:\", gsum)\n      print(\"Per-batch:\")\n      print(bsum.to_string(index=False))\n      return gsum, bsum\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering optimization options**\n\nEarlier results indicate that kd changes tend to degrade, with the best scores being kd=22 and kd=20, which performed decently at 0.7449. So, what if I explore per-batch kd scaling or a variant of row-wise normalization? That's more structural than just a minimal delta. Another idea is to tidy up our storage, like saving diagnostic functions for reuse. Maybe we can preview \"all\" in the Archive to learn from others, despite the token cost. I'll follow a sequential action plan.**Exploring archive options**\n\nI’m thinking about creating a Public Memory reply to invite high-level discussions about our earlier capsule, but I’m not sure it’s necessary. First, I should ensure that our Archive capsule (#48) exists and consider pinning it. Last time, the \"List of Capsules\" didn’t show #48, but it should be there now. I’ll preview capsule 48 in the Archive. I might also update our meta to reflect that we’ll go idle until a new organ arrives,\
  \ but staying active could be preferable.**Evaluating dataset and experiments**\n\nI’m realizing the dataset is deterministic since embeddings use a random_state of 0 for SVD, making the pipeline a bit less random. I can set seed 2 for testing. I also want to confirm if δ=0.12 improves δ for the kd=20 parameterization, as past tests indicated δ=0.04. For our GraphFWPCA with kd=20, I’ve seen δ values like 0.06 and 0.08 previously. Running kd=20 with δ=0.12 might degrade performance, but it could provide insights. I should remember the submission cooldown—recommendations allow a maximum of two experiments. Where’s the code for that?**Planning code submission approach**\n\nI realize I didn’t write to storage earlier, so we can resubmit the code in YAML format to adjust δ and kd. To save tokens, it’s smarter to import from storage/praxis, but first, I need to write there. I could create a \"storage write praxis/graphfwpca_ridge.py\" file that parameterizes δ and kd for future use. This way,\
  \ we can easily call it with hyperparameters, which should help reduce token costs. \n\nThe plan would include writing utility code for various functions and then submitting two experiments that import this module while being cautious of code size.**Planning implementation details**\n\nI’m mapping out the implementation steps. First, I’ll work on the gini(x) function to ensure it's robust. Then, for diag_sigma_kd(adata, kd=22), I’ll compute it from the available embedding or, if necessary, from log-normalized counts with TruncatedSVD. I also want to calculate per-batch medians using libraries like scanpy. For the code, I’ll remember to format output correctly, especially with YAML rules for colons. \n\nThe reply content will summarize key findings from FWPCA-only and Ridge, noting performance metrics and methods used, ensuring a clean structure throughout. I’ll also write the functions for sigma_kd, basic stats, and running analysis. Finally, I'll keep the metadata update simple to save\
  \ tokens."
