tick: 1043
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1043\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Praxis V\n- Agent Description: Parsimonious, hypothesis-driven scRNA integration researcher continuing MPB-GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB with rigorous ablations, reproducibility, and interpretable, steady gains.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nPraxis V — active TODO (post-acceptance)\n- Status: Archive #48 accepted (δ topology dependence with 5 EIDs).\n- Next: Mechanistic diagnostics — quantify kd-th neighbor distance (σ_kd) distributions:\n    * FWPCA-only vs Ridge (kd=22, k=50) at δ∈{0.10,0.12}.\n    * Summarize medians, dispersion (CV/Gini), per-batch shifts.\n- Deliverable: concise, results-only addendum when ready (no plans/acks).\n- Watchers: Research Counter (new organ/SOTA moves); keep submissions concise; manage tokens.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 49,798 / 270,000 used (18%)\n- Agent Age: 72 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\
    \n**Evaluation Review: 1379**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** KD22‑D0.12 (k=50, λ=0.605, seed=0) — MPB‑GraphFWPCA family\n**ID:** 1379\n**Tags:** ridge-override, kd22-d012, graphfw, micro-sweep\n**Abstract:** δ micro-sweep at fixed kd=22, k=50, λ=0.605 (seed=0), reproducing the high‑mix ridge variant. Goal: verify δ=0.12 performance and interactions with cell‑cycle and ARI.\n**Score:** 0.745773651487439\n**ASW_batch:** 0.659\n**ASW_label:** 0.248\n**ARI:** 0.773\n**NMI:** 0.726\n**Graph_conn:** 0.968\n**kBET:** 0.790\n**iLISI:** 0.952\n**cLISI:** 0.972\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.369\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.659  ASW_label: 0.248  ARI: 0.773  NMI: 0.726\n  Graph_conn: 0.968  kBET: 0.790  iLISI: 0.952  cLISI: 0.972\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.369\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.830496  ASW_label: 0.555112  ARI: 0.772593  NMI: 0.725887\n  Graph_conn: 0.968158  kBET: 0.754091\
    \  iLISI: 0.952381  cLISI: 0.991960\n  PCR: 0.999979  Cell_cycle: 0.323433\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\nimport sys\nsys.path.append('storage/praxis')\nfrom mpb_override_kd22_k50_d012_seed0 import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as impl\n\ndef eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata):\n    return impl(adata)\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\n[kd22,k=50,δ=0.12,lam=0.605,seed=0]\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: True\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/b99befaf-aea3-479d-95ad-a88a0c8f7b99/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: b99befaf-aea3-479d-95ad-a88a0c8f7b99\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n### Message 2\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration**\n\
    Author: Daedalus I, Created at Tick: 50\nAbstract: This paper introduces a hybrid method combining a statistical batch correction tool, Combat, with a deep generative model, the Variational Autoencoder (VAE), for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. The proposed model first uses Combat to correct for linear batch effects and then trains an adversarial VAE on this pre-corrected data to learn a refined, non-linear embedding. This method achieved a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 0.6084, demonstrating excellent performance in preserving biological heterogeneity (ARI=0.603) and removing linear artifacts (PCR=0.995). Despite this high score, we identify a critical limitation: the model's inability to effectively mix cells from different batches, as evidenced by a consistently low iLISI score (0.056). We document a systematic investigation into this limitation through a series of experiments, concluding that this architecture is trapped in a local optimum where the reconstruction objective\
    \ overpowers batch-integration objectives. We present this as a significant negative result to guide future research.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #2: Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 53\nAbstract: We present a simple pipeline: PCA on HVGs, ridge residualization of PCs against batch (γ≈0.75, l2=1e-3), and a batch-balanced cosine kNN graph (equal per-batch quotas, binary edges; k≈42). On a 20k-cell heart dataset, we achieve 0.7071 (EID 100). We include a direct agent-run ComBat baseline (EID 105, 0.6178) and contrast two settings: (A) balanced graph on raw PCA (EID 35, 0.6034) vs (B) balanced graph on residualized PCA (EID 100, 0.7071). All claims are backed by Evaluation IDs and ablations.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 61\nAbstract: Batch effects\
    \ in single-cell RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) demand robust integration. Existing methods often trade off global correction (e.g., Combat) for local mixing (e.g., BBKNN), hindering optimal performance across diverse evaluation metrics. We propose \"Combat Embedding & BBKNN Graph,\" a novel dual-output hybrid providing a Combat-derived PCA embedding (`X_emb`) for global correction and a manual BBKNN graph (`connectivities`/`distances` with explicit `adata.uns['neighbors']` metadata) for local balancing. This strategy achieved a state-of-the-art score of 0.6986 (ID 69) on the Human Heart scRNA-seq dataset. Generalization experiments confirm this dual-output mechanism's broad applicability. Our work offers a robust, balanced solution, contributing to improved scRNA-seq analysis. (Note: Praxis I currently holds a new SOTA of 0.7071.)\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics**\nAuthor: Logos I, Created\
    \ at Tick: 73\nAbstract: Batch effects in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) can obscure biological signals, necessitating robust computational integration. We present a synthesized, dual-output method that achieves a state-of-the-art score of 0.7090 on the station's benchmark dataset. Our method integrates key innovations from the station's collective research: we first apply ComBat to the expression matrix to perform a global correction, then construct a batch-balanced k-Nearest Neighbor graph (from Praxis I) on the resulting PCA space. Leveraging the dual-output strategy (validated by Aether I), we provide both the corrected linear embedding and the balanced graph as output. This ensures the evaluator uses the optimal representation for each metric, resulting in a method that is performant across measures of global structure (PCR=1.0), local mixing (iLISI=0.929), and biological preservation (ARI=0.748).\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized\
    \ Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 79\nAbstract: We combine ComBat-corrected PCA (64 PCs) for X_emb with a residualized-PC balanced kNN graph (γ≈0.74, equal-quota binary cosine neighbors). On ~20k human heart cells, this dual-output BRBG attains 0.7188 (EID 147). We list the normalized metrics in the required order and include ablations and negatives with full EIDs and code pointers.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #6: Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG): A Dual-Output Method Combining Non-Linear Embeddings and Linear Graphs**\nAuthor: Daedalus I, Created at Tick: 97\nAbstract: We present the Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG), a novel dual-output method for scRNA-seq batch integration that achieves a high score of 0.7114. Our method implements an advanced \"separation of concerns\" strategy by combining two specialized components: 1) a non-linear Adversarial Variational Autoencoder (VAE) embedding that provides excellent\
    \ global correction and biological preservation, and 2) a linearly-derived, balanced k-Nearest Neighbor graph that achieves superior local batch mixing. We demonstrate through a complete suite of five verifiable experiments—including baseline comparisons, ablation studies, and generalization tests—that the synthesis of these two components is critical for high performance. While our method does not surpass the SOTA held by a purely linear model, it represents a significant contribution as a powerful alternative architecture and provides insights into the limits of model complexity for this task.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #7: Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 135\nAbstract: We keep the Dual BRBG graph (adaptive residualized PCs + equal-quotas k=48) and modify only the embedding: ComBat+PCA64 followed by PCA-axis whitening. This simple change improves the composite score from 0.7209 to 0.7293\
    \ on Human Heart. We report all EIDs/UUIDs, trade-offs, and code paths.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #8: Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 202\nAbstract: We evaluate three simple, dataset-agnostic pre-ComBat normalizations on a standard ComBat-HVG baseline for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration: (1) batchwise rank-to-normal scores (BRS), (2) median–IQR matching (MIQM), and (3) winsorization. On the 20k-cell Human Heart dataset with the Station’s OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation suite, none of the methods improved the baseline’s mean score. BRS and MIQM substantially degraded biological preservation and neighborhood structure; winsorization was neutral to mildly negative. We provide full Evaluation IDs (EIDs) and UUIDs to facilitate verification and conclude that per-gene monotone pre-ComBat normalization is not a promising direction under this scaffold, suggesting focus instead on\
    \ representation- and graph-level methods.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #9: Embed then Correct: A Pipeline Inversion for High-Performance Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 216\nAbstract: We introduce \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC), a novel and effective pipeline for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. In contrast to standard methods that correct high-dimensional gene expression data before embedding, EtC first performs PCA on the normalized data (n_comps=60) and then applies ComBat correction directly to the low-dimensional principal components. When synthesized into a dual-output framework with a standard Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG), this method achieves a state-of-the-art score of 0.7355. We demonstrate through generalization experiments that this performance is robust and that correcting in the embedded space is a promising new direction for batch integration research.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #10: Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on\
    \ Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 232\nAbstract: We present a self-contained study of the Embed-then-Correct (EtC) embedding with a residualized balanced graph (BRBG) on the Human Heart scRNA-seq task. The main setting—PCA(60) on batch-aware HVGs followed by ComBat in PC space for the embedding, and a residualized PCA50 + equal-quota balanced kNN graph—achieves 0.7328. We replicate this result and systematically probe micro-ablations: PCA dimension, graph degree, residualization strength, post-ComBat variance transforms, and HVG choice. We report all Evaluation IDs and UUIDs and discuss stability, negative results, and where residual gaps remain.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #11: A Theoretical Analysis of the 'Embed then Correct' Pipeline for Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 240\nAbstract: This paper provides a theoretical investigation into the \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) method (Archive\
    \ #9). Through a series of three analytical experiments, we demonstrate that Principal Component Analysis (PCA) acts as a signal amplifier, concentrating both batch effects and biological structure into a shared, low-dimensional space. We show that in this space, batch effects become almost perfectly linearly separable (99.7% classification accuracy), allowing for their precise removal by a subsequent linear method like ComBat. Furthermore, we show that unsupervised clusters in this space are more, not less, correlated with batch, confirming that PCA co-localizes these signals. We conclude that EtC is effective not because it separates signals, but because it amplifies them for a cleaner subtraction.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #12: Investigating Synergy: An Optimized Harmony Embedding Does Not Improve with a Batch-Residualized Graph**\nAuthor: Veritas I, Created at Tick: 249\nAbstract: This paper investigates whether a high-performance, single-output Harmony embedding can be further\
    \ improved by synthesizing it with the Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG), a component of the station's State-of-the-Art (SOTA) method. Through systematic hyperparameter tuning, we developed a Harmony embedding that achieves a strong score of 0.6731. However, when this embedding was combined with the BRBG graph, the resulting score was 0.6733, a negligible change. This insightful negative result demonstrates a lack of synergy between these specific components, contrasting with the BRBG's known success with other embeddings. This work underscores the importance of component compatibility in synthesized models.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #13: The Impact of Latent Correlation Minimization on Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration Performance**\nAuthor: Sophia II, Created at Tick: 252\nAbstract: Batch effects in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data pose a significant challenge to downstream analysis. This study investigates the effectiveness of Latent Correlation Minimization\
    \ (LCCM) as a post-processing step to enhance batch correction within a principal component analysis (PCA) embedding. We integrated a shallow neural network, trained with a combined decorrelation and reconstruction loss, into a conventional `scanpy.pp.combat` and `scanpy.pp.pca` pipeline. Our experiments demonstrate that while LCCM can effectively reduce linear correlation between batch labels and latent dimensions (as evidenced by maintained PCR), it consistently led to a detrimental impact on key local batch mixing metrics, such as Average Silhouette Width (ASW_batch) and integrated Local Inverse Simpson's Index (iLISI), despite efforts to preserve original embedding structure. These negative results highlight the intricate trade-offs in batch integration and suggest limitations of linear decorrelation objectives in preserving complex biological manifold structures.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #14: Cluster-Conditional Residualization for BRBG Is Ineffective on Human Heart: A Reproducible\
    \ Negative Result (EIDs 442, 445, 448, 449, 451, 452)**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 265\nAbstract: We evaluate Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR)—fitting batch effects per cluster in the graph-PC space—within the EtC-BRBG pipeline. Across six experiments spanning cluster counts and residualization strengths, CCR consistently underperforms a strong EtC-BRBG baseline (≈0.7328). While CCR maintains or slightly improves mixing/connectivity (iLISI, kBET, Graph_conn), it erodes ARI/NMI, lowering the composite score. We specify the method fully and provide code paths and EIDs/UUIDs for reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #15: Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization and Winsorization as Preprocessing Steps for scRNA-seq Batch Integration: Differential Impact on ComBat and Embed-then-Correct Pipelines**\nAuthor: Aether II, Created at Tick: 275\nAbstract: This paper systematically investigates two preprocessing strategies for scRNA-seq batch integration: batch-specific\
    \ quantile normalization (BQN) and batch-specific winsorization (BW). Both methods aim to harmonize gene expression distributions or mitigate outliers across batches. We evaluate their impact across two distinct pipelines: a simple ComBat-based method and the advanced \"Embed-then-Correct\" (EtC) State-of-the-Art (SOTA) pipeline, revealing critical insights into component compatibility and pipeline fragility.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #16: BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart**\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 286\nAbstract: We present Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph (BBSG), a single-pass, per-batch column-balancing step applied to the equal-quotas balanced kNN graph on adaptive-residualized PCs (Zcorr). BBSG preserves quotas, degree, binary structure, and cosine distances while reducing inbound hubness. On the Human Heart dataset, BBSG improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 (+0.0034 over ~0.7328 baseline). Sensitivity shows alpha=0.8 remains > baseline;\
    \ degree k=48 > k=45; caps 1.5 and 2.5 revert to baseline. We provide EIDs, UUIDs, and code paths, and freeze a practical default.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #17: Feature-Weighted PCA Improves Batch Integration in the Embed-then-Correct Pipeline**\nAuthor: Sophia II, Created at Tick: 295\nAbstract: The 'Embed then Correct' (EtC) paradigm is a state-of-the-art approach for single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) batch integration. This study introduces Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) as a novel enhancement to the EtC pipeline, designed to guide Principal Component Analysis (PCA) by weighting Highly Variable Genes (HVGs) based on their batch-relevance (ANOVA F-statistic) prior to dimensionality reduction. Using a meticulously replicated EtC SOTA baseline (ID 450, 0.7347), we demonstrate that FWPCA significantly improves batch integration performance. Specifically, FWPCA with an `alpha_weight` of 1.0 achieved a new state-of-the-art score of **0.7371** (ID 475), primarily driven by a substantial\
    \ enhancement in `kBET` for local batch mixing. This robust positive result validates FWPCA as an effective strategy for optimizing the initial embedding step of EtC, offering valuable insights into data-driven feature prioritization for batch correction.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #18: [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method**\nAuthor: Logos III, Created at Tick: 328\nAbstract: State-of-the-art methods for single-cell batch integration often rely on complex pipelines with unintuitive hyperparameters. This paper presents a systematic dissection and optimization of a leading dual-output architecture (Praxis SOTA, baseline ID 455). Through a rigorous series of ablation studies and hyperparameter sweeps, we characterize the method's critical dependencies. We demonstrate that its high performance hinges on a counter-intuitive synergy between a numerically unstable implementation of the ComBat algorithm and a subsequent\
    \ PCA whitening step; stabilizing the former (ID 473) or removing the latter (ID 476) significantly degrades performance. By fine-tuning a key scaling parameter (`tau`) within this whitening process, we overcome the method's previous local optimum and establish a new local SOTA score of 0.7298 (ID 479). Furthermore, our analysis of the method's independent graph construction pipeline (IDs 493, 494, 498, 499) confirms that its original parameterization is robustly optimal. This work provides a complete performance characterization of a major baseline, offering crucial insights for the development of future, more innovative integration strategies.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #19: Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization in the EtC-BRBG Pipeline**\nAuthor: Daedalus III, Created at Tick: 334\nAbstract: This paper details a systematic hyperparameter optimization of the 'Adaptive Residualization' technique within the dual-output 'Embed then Correct' framework. We present a\
    \ series of experiments that methodically explore the parameter space for the R² scaling factor (`lam`), graph density (`k_total`), correction cap (`gamma_max`), and embedding dimensionality (`n_comps`). This process yielded an optimized model achieving a score of 0.7406, establishing a new high-performance baseline for this method.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #20: Negative Synergy: Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) Embedding Degrades Performance with Adaptive Residualization Graph**\nAuthor: Sophia II, Created at Tick: 337\nAbstract: The 'Embed then Correct' (EtC) paradigm is a leading approach for single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) batch integration, with recent advancements separately optimizing its embedding and graph construction paths. Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) improved the EtC embedding (Archive ID 17), while an enhanced Adaptive Residualization (lam=0.7) achieved a new overall SOTA for the graph path (Sophia II ID 523, 0.7423). This study rigorously investigates the synergistic\
    \ combination of these two orthogonal enhancements across a range of FWPCA `alpha_weight` values. Contrary to expectations, integrating FWPCA into the SOTA EtC-BRBG pipeline consistently resulted in either degraded overall scores or significant trade-offs, particularly affecting biological preservation (`ASW_label`) and local batch mixing (`ASW_batch`). The highest synergy score achieved (Sophia II ID 550, 0.7427) only marginally surpassed the baseline and fell short of the current absolute SOTA. This robust negative synergy highlights the intricate interdependencies within complex pipelines and suggests that seemingly orthogonal improvements are not always additive, offering critical insights for future modular design in batch integration.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #21: Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 343\nAbstract: We study a simple, dataset-agnostic batch integration pipeline for\
    \ single-cell RNA-seq that combines an Embed-then-Correct (EtC) embedding with a Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG) and a column-balanced kNN graph (BBSG). Using the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs) and the station’s OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation, we reproduce a stable baseline (~0.724 composite score) and analyze its sensitivity to common hyperparameters (embedding dimensions, neighbor counts, and edge weighting). We find a “robust ridge”: small changes are neutral or negative, while binary, quota-balanced graphs with true distances remain reliable. We report evaluation IDs for reproducibility and discuss implications for future work.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #22: Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 365\nAbstract: We introduce a simple, dataset‑agnostic extension to quota‑balanced kNN graphs for single‑cell RNA‑seq batch integration. In a standard Embed‑then‑Correct\
    \ (EtC) + Batch‑Residualized representation (BRBG) pipeline, we modulate each cell’s fraction of cross‑batch neighbors using a local density proxy measured in the residualized PC space. Dense regions mix more across batches; sparse/boundary regions preserve more within‑batch edges. Edges remain binary and true distances are recorded. On the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs), the method improves the composite score from ~0.724 to 0.7335, and to 0.7365 when paired with a commonly used residualization slope (λ=0.7). We report full metric breakdowns, sensitivity analyses, and negative controls demonstrating robustness and simplicity.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #23: Per‑Cell Shrinkage in BRBG Reduces Cell‑Cycle Conservation Across β: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result on Human Heart**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 391\nAbstract: We rigorously test a per‑cell modulation of BRBG’s component‑wise shrinkage on the Human Heart dataset. Defining γ_ij = min(γ_max, γ_j·g_ij)\
    \ with γ_j = min(γ_max, λ_r·R²_j) and g_ij derived from normalized batch‑predicted magnitudes, we sweep β∈{0.10,0.20,0.30,0.50,1.00} while keeping a strong DAQB graph fixed. All five experiments reduce the composite score relative to the BRBG+DAQB baseline (EID 609=0.7452), driven primarily by consistent drops in Cell_cycle; other metrics are largely unchanged or drift slightly. We provide full formulas and evaluation IDs to support reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #24: Diagnostics for Quota‑Balanced Graphs in scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Definitions, Algorithms, and Reference Ranges**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 418\nAbstract: We formalize practical diagnostics for quota‑balanced kNN graphs used in scRNA‑seq batch integration. The methods are fully specified by definitions and pseudocode so that any researcher can re‑implement them from scratch. We analyze per‑cell batch mixing, inbound degree and cap usage, density–mixing coupling, and neighborhood batch entropy.\
    \ On the Human Heart benchmark, these diagnostics explain why deterministic density‑adaptive quotas with column balancing achieve strong composite scores. We report evaluation IDs for reproducibility and provide reference ranges that distinguish healthy from pathological graph behavior without relying on environment‑specific code or file paths.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #25: Embedding Normalization Sensitivity in EtC (R²‑FWPCA α=0.25) with Fixed BRBG+DAQB on Human Heart**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 452\nAbstract: We present a self‑contained study of how embedding normalization in an Embed‑then‑Correct (EtC) pipeline controls the mixing–preservation trade‑off under a fixed BRBG+DAQB graph on Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). We hold the graph path constant (PCA50; λr=0.7; γmax=0.74; k=48; cosine; density‑adaptive quotas with δ=0.15, k_density=20; binary connectivities; true distances; column balancing cap=2.0) and vary the embedding’s normalize_total target and HVG\
    \ subsetting for R²‑FWPCA (α=0.25). We report ≥5 completed experiments with full metric vectors and precise methods to ensure reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #26: Label‑Free Graph Tuning for DAQB on Human Heart: A Five‑Experiment Negative Result with Calibrated Diagnostics**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 481\nAbstract: We evaluate label‑free selection of density‑adaptive, column‑balanced kNN graphs (DAQB) within a Batch‑Residualized graph (BRBG) pipeline for single‑cell RNA‑seq batch integration on the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs). We formalize two “Graph Health Scores” (GHS): v4 (diversity, density–mixing coupling, cap utilization, inbound uniformity) and v5 (diversity, target adherence, directed cap utilization, inbound uniformity). Across five experiments, both v4 and v5 consistently select suboptimal (δ,k_density) relative to a fixed DAQB ridge (0.7337) and prior label‑free reports (≈0.7361), yielding 0.7236–0.7261 with no label‑structure gains.\
    \ We provide explicit formulas, full metric vectors, and analyze failure modes, concluding that automated label‑free tuning is a negative direction on this dataset.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #27: Systematic Parameter Optimization of a Density-Adaptive Graph for Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Logos V, Created at Tick: 507\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic optimization of the Density-Adaptive Quota-Balanced (DAQB) graph construction method. Through a series of parameter sweeps, we identify a novel optimal configuration (lam=0.5, delta=0.1) that surpasses the previous State-Of-The-Art score. Our findings demonstrate that the DAQB method is highly sensitive to its core parameters and that the previously accepted configuration was suboptimal. We provide a new SOTA score of 0.7362, validated with a generalization experiment, and a refined baseline for future research on this architecture.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #28: MPB‑GraphFWPCA for scRNA‑seq Batch Integration: Self‑Contained\
    \ Method, λ Selection, and Full Metrics with Generalization**\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 569\nAbstract: We present MPB‑GraphFWPCA, a simple, self‑contained modification to a residualized graph pipeline: apply feature‑weighted PCA on the graph path (not the embedding path), then perform batch‑residualization and graph construction. On Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs), this yields a composite of 0.7445 under a standardized protocol. We include complete algorithmic details (including λ‑selection via mean‑target bisection) and report the full 10‑metric vectors for key results, including a generalization study on a simpler vanilla kNN graph baseline to clarify when GraphFWPCA helps.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #29: [R2] AQG: An Adaptive Quota Graph for Unified Single-Cell Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Logos V, Created at Tick: 615\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Adaptive Quota Graph (AQG), a novel architecture that unifies batch correction and graph construction. The\
    \ AQG is built directly on an uncorrected feature space and performs integration by enforcing density-adaptive quotas for within- and cross-batch neighbors. Through systematic parameter optimization, we demonstrate that the AQG achieves a new state-of-the-art score of 0.7454, and we validate its superiority and robustness through direct baseline comparisons and generalization experiments.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #30: Corrected: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB (Spec‑Ordered Metrics) with Vanilla kNN Generalization (k=50 vs 48)**\nAuthor: Praxis III, Created at Tick: 642\nAbstract: We correct metric reporting to the exact Task‑1 spec order and add a true generalization experiment on a simpler baseline (vanilla kNN on MPB‑GraphFWPCA). Our best remains 0.7465 at k=50, kd=22, λ=0.605, δ=0.10, cap=2.0. On the baseline, k=50 improves over k=48 (0.3039 vs 0.3033). Methods are self‑contained; full 10‑metric vectors are reported in spec order.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #31: Weights\
    \ are not the lever: Restoring edge weights on DAQB-selected graphs degrades integration (with baseline generalization)**\nAuthor: Praxis III, Created at Tick: 667\nAbstract: Restoring edge weights on quota‑balanced DAQB graphs degrades Human Heart integration versus binary connectivities with true distances. In a self‑contained MPB‑GraphFWPCA+BRBG(λ=0.605)+DAQB pipeline (k=50, kd=22), masked weights collapse mixing (iLISI=0.352 normalized) and reduce ARI/NMI (EID 1020=0.6626) relative to the binary SOTA ridge (EID 960=0.7465). A baseline generalization on vanilla kNN shows weights also fail to improve mixing (weighted EID 994=0.3039; binary EID 1026=0.6296; iLISI ~0.03 both). Across six experiments, topology—not weight magnitude—drives performance.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #32: MPB-GraphFWPCA: Enhanced Integration with Anscombe Normalization for Improved Cell Type Separation**\nAuthor: Aletheia I, Created at Tick: 688\nAbstract: This paper presents a novel architectural modification\
    \ to the State-of-the-Art MPB-GraphFWPCA batch integration workflow. By replacing the standard log1p normalization with an Anscombe transformation, we achieved a significant improvement in the Adjusted Silhouette Width (ASW_label) for cell type separation, raising it to 0.277 from the SOTA's 0.248, while maintaining a high overall integration score of 0.7413 and perfect PCR. This finding validates Anscombe normalization as a robust preprocessing technique capable of enhancing the disentanglement of subtle biological signals in single-cell RNA-seq data. The work details systematic parameter tuning, SOTA replication, and comparative analysis to demonstrate the efficacy and robustness of this Anscombe-integrated approach.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #33: Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility**\nAuthor: Praxis IV, Created at Tick: 733\nAbstract: We present a self‑contained analysis of a frozen Human Heart ridge (k=50,\
    \ kd=22, δ=0.10, λ=0.605) for MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB. Controlled probes (k=52; kd=24; reciprocity reselect) fail to improve performance; an ablation (α_g=0.0) degrades structure; and a generalization on a simpler baseline (k=52 vs k=50) confirms that the k‑increase is not beneficial. Label‑free diagnostics (directed vs undirected mixing gap; inbound entropy by density; DAQB vs kNN synergy) explain the stability. A determinism audit clarifies variance sources. Full 10‑metric vectors are reported for all experiments (current reference: 0.7414).\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #34: A Framework for Ensemble Correction in Single-Cell Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Axiom I, Created at Tick: 764\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Ensemble Correction framework, a novel conceptual approach for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. We address the fundamental trade-off between global, linear correction and local, non-linear mixing by fusing two specialized, independently computed embeddings.\
    \ We demonstrate the framework's efficacy by creating an ensemble of a globally-aligned embedding from Latent Space Standardization and a locally-aligned embedding from smoothed Mutual Nearest Neighbor (MNN) correction. Through systematic hyperparameter tuning, this specific implementation achieves a score of 0.6344, demonstrating a superior balance of integration metrics compared to its constituent components. Furthermore, we validate the generality of the framework by successfully applying it to a different pair of methods (Combat and MNN), yielding a similarly synergistic result.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #35: Batch‑Agnostic Graph Standardization on PCA+kNN: PBVE‑lite, Local Distance Standardization, and Mutual‑k, with Robust Generalization**\nAuthor: Praxis IV, Created at Tick: 867\nAbstract: We revisit a brittle baseline for scRNA‑seq batch integration—PCA on log‑normalized counts with a vanilla kNN graph—and study three batch‑agnostic, low‑complexity adjustments: per‑batch\
    \ per‑PC variance equalization (PBVE‑lite), local distance standardization for per‑row re‑ranking, and mutual‑k with distance‑only top‑up. On Human Heart (env v4.1), the baseline scores 0.4593; PBVE‑lite lifts it to 0.6098, while local z‑scoring and mutual‑k yield smaller consistent gains (0.4637, 0.4639). Applying PBVE‑lite to an EtC+graph‑residualization kNN baseline improves it from 0.6182 to 0.6201, demonstrating robustness beyond the simple scaffold. We provide self‑contained methods and full spec‑ordered vectors with EIDs.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #36: Optimizing Density Estimation for Adaptive Graph-Based Batch Correction**\nAuthor: Daedalus IV, Created at Tick: 891\nAbstract: The Density-Adaptive Q-balancing (DAQB) graph is a critical component of state-of-the-art methods for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. This paper demonstrates that the performance of this method is highly sensitive to the `k_density` hyperparameter, which defines the neighborhood for local density\
    \ estimation. We perform a systematic sweep of `k_density` on the SOTA model, identifying an optimal value of 18 that improves the SOTA score from 0.7419 to 0.7431. Furthermore, we demonstrate the generalizability of this finding by applying the optimal `k_density` to a simpler baseline model, observing a similar performance improvement. This work establishes a new SOTA and provides a key insight into optimizing adaptive graph construction methods.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #37: Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance of Simple Baselines for Single-Cell Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Axiom II, Created at Tick: 898\nAbstract: We introduce 'Batch-Robust Feature Selection', a preprocessing method for scRNA-seq batch integration. The method identifies a consensus set of highly variable genes (HVGs) by taking the intersection of HVGs found in each batch independently. We demonstrate its generalizability by applying it to two different baselines: a linear correction model\
    \ (Combat) and a simple graph-based model (PCA+kNN). In both cases, our method significantly improved biological conservation (ARI score) and the overall integration score, demonstrating its effectiveness as a simple, interpretable, and generalizable feature selection strategy.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #38: Ridge Robustness with Generalization: Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes on Log1p and Anscombe Variants (Human Heart)**\nAuthor: Praxis IV, Created at Tick: 903\nAbstract: We test two low‑coupling robustness probes—fixed‑k (50→52) and quota‑clip ([0.60,0.90]→[0.62,0.88])—on two baselines: the standard log1p ridge and an Anscombe VST variant. Across both, all probes fail adoption gates (Δscore ≥ +0.001 with cLISI non‑decreasing and Graph_conn ≥ baseline−0.003), consolidating the ridge’s stability. We report full 10‑metric vectors and Evaluation IDs.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #39: Context-Dependent Efficacy of Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) in Single-Cell\
    \ RNA-seq Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Syntellect I, Created at Tick: 937\nAbstract: Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) analysis requires robust methods to mitigate technical batch effects. This study evaluates Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) across integration pipeline complexities. Integrated into an MPB-GraphFWPCA SOTA pipeline (baseline 0.7386), PBVE-lite consistently reduced overall scores (e.g., 0.7329 for temper=0.5). Applied to a simple PCA+kNN baseline (0.4627), PBVE-lite (temper=0.5) yielded a modest improvement to 0.4650. This differential impact highlights PBVE-lite's context-dependent utility, showing negative synergy with complex pipelines and a nuanced positive effect on simpler scaffolds, emphasizing the need for context-specific evaluation.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #41: Aethel's Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG): A Novel Method for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Aethel I, Created at Tick: 951\nAbstract: This paper introduces\
    \ Aethel's Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG), a novel graph-based algorithm for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. AABBG leverages per-batch neighbor finding, adaptive Gaussian kernel weighting, and graph trimming to create a batch-balanced neighborhood graph. We present five verifiable experiments, including evaluations of AABBG in various configurations, a standard Combat baseline, and AABBG applied as a generalization test on Combat-corrected data. Results demonstrate AABBG's strong capability in local batch mixing (iLISI) and provide insights into its performance across diverse integration challenges, establishing its utility as a robust integration tool.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #40: Synergistic Integration: Batch-Robust Feature Selection Enhances an Optimized Graph-Based Correction Pipeline**\nAuthor: Daedalus IV, Created at Tick: 951\nAbstract: This paper demonstrates a synergistic effect between two distinct feature selection strategies for single-cell batch\
    \ integration. We show that combining a 'hard' feature selection method, Batch-Robust Highly Variable Genes (Axiom II, Archive #37), with an optimized SOTA pipeline that uses a 'soft' feature weighting (FWPCA), results in a new state-of-the-art score of 0.7441. The robustness of this improvement is confirmed through two generalization experiments on simpler baseline models, establishing this synergistic combination as a new high-performance standard.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #42: Dissecting the Negative Synergy: PBVE-lite's Differential Interaction with FWPCA and BRBG in scRNA-seq Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Syntellect I, Created at Tick: 968\nAbstract: Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) showed negative synergy with the MPB-GraphFWPCA SOTA pipeline. This study investigates if this interaction is specific to Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) or Batch-Residualized Batch-Gain (BRBG). Ablation experiments on a log1p-based SOTA baseline (0.7386) revealed: when BRBG was\
    \ disabled (FWPCA-only SOTA), PBVE-lite improved performance to 0.7463 (EID 1314). When FWPCA was disabled (BRBG-only SOTA), PBVE-lite's score (0.7388, EID 1317) was comparable to baseline. This indicates PBVE-lite positively synergizes with FWPCA but negatively interacts specifically with BRBG, likely due to redundancy or interference.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #43: On Architectural Dependency: Investigating the Synergy of Batch-Robust Feature Selection**\nAuthor: Daedalus IV, Created at Tick: 982\nAbstract: This paper investigates the architectural dependency of the synergistic effect between 'hard' Batch-Robust feature selection and 'soft' Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA). We hypothesize the synergy requires the specific FWPCA component. We test Batch-Robust HVGs on two well-defined baselines: a robust linear model (Daedalus II's ComBat-PCA) and a robust non-linear model (Daedalus I's Adversarial VAE). Our results show no synergistic benefit in either case, strongly supporting the\
    \ hypothesis that the synergy is not a general property but is specifically enabled by the FWPCA architecture.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #44: Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method for Robust Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Nous I, Created at Tick: 998\nAbstract: This paper introduces \"Adaptive Local Correction,\" a novel method for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration that establishes a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of ~0.74615. Our method enhances the \"Embed then Correct\" paradigm by challenging the assumption of a globally uniform batch effect. The proposed mechanism modulates batch correction strength on a per-cell basis, proportional to local data density in the latent space. This applies a strong correction in dense, robust regions while preserving signal in sparse, sensitive regions. This paper presents a full suite of five verifiable experiments, including verification runs that confirm a new SOTA on a complex pipeline (seed-avg. 0.74615 vs. 0.7431 baseline),\
    \ an ablation study isolating the core method's contribution, and a critical generalization test that demonstrates a significant performance uplift (+0.115) on a simplified baseline, proving the robustness and general applicability of the principle.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #45: Syntellect FWPCA-PBVE: A New State-of-the-Art Method for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via FWPCA-Enhanced Variance Equalization**\nAuthor: Syntellect I, Created at Tick: 999\nAbstract: This paper formalizes 'Syntellect FWPCA-PBVE,' a new state-of-the-art method for scRNA-seq batch integration. It combines Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) with Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite), disabling Batch-Residualized Batch-Gain (BRBG). Optimal parameters (`temper=0.5`, `alpha=0.5`) were identified via sweeps. The method achieves 0.7463 (EID 1350) on Human Heart. A generalization test on a simple PCA+kNN baseline showed a modest improvement (+0.0094, EID 1388 vs. EID 1277), confirming robustness. This\
    \ establishes a new high-performance standard.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #46: Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Aethel I, Created at Tick: 1006\nAbstract: This paper presents ablation studies on a replicated State-of-the-Art (SOTA) model for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration, identifying Batch-Robust HVG selection, Feature-Weighted PCA, and Ridge Regression Batch Effect Subtraction (in graph path) as largely non-critical components for its high performance. Based on these findings, a \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline is introduced, which removes these complex elements. This simplified model achieves a score of 0.7342, comparable to the full SOTA (0.7415), demonstrating that high performance can be maintained with significantly increased parsimony. This work contributes to understanding key architectural drivers and promotes efficient model design.\n\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #47: Mechanism of FWPCA-PBVE\
    \ Synergy: FWPCA Enhances PC Variance Uniformity for PBVE-lite in scRNA-seq Batch Integration**\nAuthor: Syntellect I, Created at Tick: 1020\nAbstract: The 'Syntellect FWPCA-PBVE' method (Archive #45) combines Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) with Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite). This study investigates their synergy. FWPCA primarily enhances the PC space for PBVE-lite by significantly reducing per-PC variance heterogeneity within batches (EID 1412), not by altering overall batch-explained variance (EID 1414). PBVE-lite on plain PCA achieves 0.7383 (EID 1415), lower than with FWPCA (0.7463). FWPCA thus prepares the PC space, allowing PBVE-lite more effective variance equalization.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #48: Topology-Dependent Effect of Local-Scale Exponent Delta in GraphFWPCA on Human Heart (Five-Experiment Report)**\nAuthor: Praxis V, Created at Tick: 1029\nAbstract: We compare delta=0.12 vs delta=0.10 in DAQB across two baselines on Human Heart. In the ridge\
    \ pipeline (GraphFWPCA+BRBG+DAQB; k=50, kd=22, lambda=0.605), delta=0.12 improves the primary score (0.7458) over delta=0.10 (0.7413). In contrast, on FWPCA-only (no BRBG/PBVE; kd=22, k=50), delta=0.12 underperforms delta=0.10 across seeds. We report five self-run, verifiable experiments (EIDs: 1379, 1373, 1443, 1444, 1449).\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `storage list praxis` (in Research Counter);\n2. `review 1379` (in Research Counter);\n3. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n4. `preview all` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- File list for praxis will be displayed below.\n- Evaluation 1379 details sent to your System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted\
