thinking_content: "**Considering KNN Improvements**\n\nI'm contemplating alternatives for kBET iLISI. To enhance it, we might want to focus on using a per-batch neighbor search instead of applying quotas to a global neighbor list. This approach guarantees the existence of cross-batch neighbors. The standard solution, like BBKNN, opts for selecting neighbors per batch, choosing a specific number based on batch size. While this method might be heavier, it allows for separate neighbor models to be computed for each batch, which seems acceptable in this context.**Implementing BBKNN Neighbor Assignment**\n\nI'm planning to implement neighbor assignment similar to BBKNN. For each batch, I’ll fit the NearestNeighbors on its cells, aiming to select cross-batch neighbors. Rather than being confined to in-batch neighbors, BBKNN identifies each cell's k neighbors from all batches. By calculating neighbors for cell i among the selected batch, we can achieve equal quotas. This approach will produce aggregated\
  \ W_dir edges across batches, potentially enhancing iLISI. I could also explore adding column balancing to maintain uniform inbound quotas while ensuring degree distribution isn't skewed.**Preparing for Improved Algorithms**\n\nI'm thinking about replicating older results using the EtC baseline. Previously, the synergy pipeline relied on a residualized PC graph, which wasn't based on equal quotas. However, my minimal baseline is set with equal quotas. To enhance this, I plan to run the improved \"balanced per-batch neighbor search,\" which is more robust. I’ll submit \"BBKNN-minimal EtC\" with this update. While we wait for results from our current submissions, I'm preparing the code for future use, including a \"balanced_graph.py\" utility for testing. I need to keep an eye on code length and my token budget.**Planning Next Steps**\n\nI'm considering implementing a robust equal-quota method for the per-batch approach. My next action involves submitting a new evaluation using this robust\
  \ algorithm with k set to 50 and possibly using a cosine metric, as that's historically been favored in SOTA graphs. Although Euclidean distances are often recommended, past Praxis results showed variations like k=48. Since cosine distances were used in earlier baselines, switching to cosine could improve mixing in the EtC pipelines, aligning with past methodologies.**Planning Future Submissions**\n\nI'm analyzing the earlier Lumen results, which averaged 0.5938. By switching to a robust equal quota with cosine metrics, I project we could improve to around 0.72. I’m proposing a new submission titled \"EtC BRBG minimal v3\" that includes the per-batch neighbor search with cosine metrics. Implementation will involve using NearestNeighbors to compute neighbors separately for each batch while maintaining equal quotas. I must watch our current submissions, as we can only run two evaluations concurrently, but I’m preparing storage modules and calculating the next steps for additional cosine\
  \ runs.**Planning Actions and Reflections**\n\nI want to optimize our algorithm's speed for 20k cells with 60 dimensions. While k_neighbors set at 257 might feel heavy, it should work well enough. For now, here are my next steps:\n\nI’ll create storage for the 'equal_quota_graph.py' to share for future runs, update my private watchlist, and plan the v3-cosine submission. I also need to ensure 'neighbors metadata' is included and check the status of submissions 1497 and 1498 before we proceed. Finally, I will issue commands to review both submissions. I'll keep it concise. \n\nHere are the commands:\n- /execute_action{review 1497}\n- /execute_action{review 1498}\n\nReflection: We might fix under-mixing with candidate expansion and consider using the cosine metric next."
