thinking_content: "**Evaluating metrics and results**\n\nI tried the cosine metric with equal-quota, and I got a score of 0.67937 with specific metrics showing improvement. My ARI is at 0.795 and NMI at 0.744. Overall, that's the best at 0.6794 in our minimal line, though it's still below the state-of-the-art around 0.745. We also submitted some candidates; now, I need to decide whether to integrate GraphFWPCA or something more advanced. I should plan to review results when they're ready.**Planning further experiments**\n\nNext, we can't review the pending results, but I can propose more experiments. Should we try candidates even higher? Our previous equal-quota change to candidate 384 saw improvements, but we used 256 for the cosine metric; maybe going to 512 could be beneficial, though it requires heavy computation.\n\nI have some adjustments to consider, like setting k values at 48 versus 50; k48 had a slight drop before. Also, I should look into neighbor distances for cosine metrics\
  \ and decide between binary and weighted connectivities.**Exploring potential adjustments**\n\nI’ve noticed that weighted options seldom help, so I’m considering some other tweaks. One option is adjusting the embedding dimension to 60 versus 50, which could impact performance if we use HPC.\n\nAnother thought is to apply GraphFWPCA on the graph path instead of relying on residualization, though that brings us close to the ridge of MPB GraphFWPCA we discussed earlier. \n\nWe could also try replicating Aethel's state-of-the-art \"Adaptive Local Correction\" pipeline, keeping our independence in mind. Additionally, I’m contemplating the implementation of a \"quota-clip\" symmetrical union. But first, I might try a \"reciprocal top-up,\" considering previous results showed issues with ties degrading performance. Lastly, running small sweeps around the best minimal v4 with cosine appears to be a solid plan.**Considering parameter adjustments**\n\nI’m weighing different parameters like λ_r values\
  \ of 0.65, 0.7, and 0.6, along with candidate counts of 256, 384, and 512. I’ve noticed k values of 48 versus 50 and that kd in quotas hasn’t affected equal-quota, though DAQB-lite uses kd. \n\nFor the minimal v4, we used equal-quota, but testing kd in density normalization could be insightful. \n\nI think we should try new PCA components for the graph path, experimenting with 62 versus 50. We should submit two new runs: \"v5b\" with λ_r 0.70 and \"v5c\" with k 48 and keep moving forward with our plans!**Planning submission and reviews**\n\nI’m looking at running experiments with cosine equal-quota settings. One option is λ_r at 0.65, k at 48, and candidates at 384. We might also want to consider normalizing graph vectors to unit norm before using cosine, as it ensures metric consistency without needing to manually L2 normalize.\n\nFor metrics, integrating improvements from previous successes, like the increases in Graph_conn and iLISI, is on my mind. I want to design experiments using\
  \ candidates of 512 and 384, with careful consideration of concurrency, as I can only run two evaluations simultaneously. \n\nRight now, I plan to wait for the current experiments to finish and prepare for future submissions. I'll also set watchers and keep track of results as they come in. I'll log a minimal plan in private memory and queue the upcoming submissions as needed later on."
