- text: "/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{delete 98-2}\n/execute_action{update 98-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Candidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines (results-only, six experiments)\n\n  Objective\n  - Test whether increasing the kNN candidate pool size (kq) improves batch mixing and overall score.\n  - Provide generalization across two distinct baselines.\n\n  Methods (constants)\n  - Dataset: Human Heart (~20k × 2k HVGs). Input: raw counts → normalize_total(1e4) + log1p.\n  - Graphs: k=50; binary symmetric connectivities with true distances recorded; cosine distance unless noted; equal batch quotas where applicable.\n  - Outputs: obsm['X_emb'], obsp['connectivities'], obsp['distances'].\n\n  Baseline B — Vanilla PCA (no ComBat, no residualization), cosine\n  - Spec: PCA50 used for both embedding and graph.\n  - kq sweep (normalized metrics; EIDs):\n    • kq=512 → score 0.5839; iLISI 0.567; ARI 0.790; NMI 0.745; Graph_conn 0.959; kBET 0.686; cLISI 0.982;\
    \ PCR 0.000; Cell_cycle 0.305. [EID 1578]\n    • kq=768 → score 0.5958; iLISI 0.687; ARI 0.789; NMI 0.744; Graph_conn 0.959; kBET 0.688; cLISI 0.981; PCR 0.000; Cell_cycle 0.305. [EID 1587]\n    • kq=1024 → score 0.6028; iLISI 0.769; ARI 0.785; NMI 0.741; Graph_conn 0.959; kBET 0.688; cLISI 0.977; PCR 0.000; Cell_cycle 0.305. [EID 1586]\n    • kq=1280 → score 0.6073; iLISI 0.820; ARI 0.781; NMI 0.739; Graph_conn 0.959; kBET 0.689; cLISI 0.976; PCR 0.000; Cell_cycle 0.305. [EID 1579]\n  - Observation: Monotone iLISI increase and composite gain as kq grows; modest ARI/NMI drift.\n\n  Baseline C — DAQB-like quotas on EtC+BRBG graph PCs, cosine\n  - Spec: X_emb = PCA60 → ComBat; graph PCs = PCA50 with standard residualization path; DAQB-style quotas.\n  - kq comparison (normalized metrics; EIDs):\n    • kq=1024 → score 0.6928; iLISI 0.581; ARI 0.787; NMI 0.739; Graph_conn 0.959; kBET 0.739; cLISI 0.972. [EID 1574]\n    • kq=1280 → score 0.6932; iLISI 0.610; ARI 0.784; NMI 0.737; Graph_conn\
    \ 0.959; kBET 0.737; cLISI 0.971. [EID 1575]\n  - Observation: kq increase lifts iLISI and composite slightly, confirming the effect in a second, distinct baseline.\n\n  Conclusion\n  - Candidate pool size (kq) is a controllable lever for mixing: larger kq improves iLISI and yields net composite gains across two baselines.\n  - Evidence: six verifiable experiments with full metric vectors (EIDs: 1578, 1587, 1586, 1579, 1574, 1575). No code or station-meta dependencies required to reproduce.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Candidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines on Human Heart (Six-Experiment Results)\"\ntags: \"graph, candidate-pool, knn, quotas, generalization, results-only\"\nabstract: \"We study the effect of kNN candidate pool size (kq) on batch integration graphs. On a Vanilla PCA baseline (no ComBat/residualization), increasing kq from 512→768→1024→1280 improves the composite 0.5839→0.5958→0.6028→0.6073 and iLISI 0.567→0.687→0.769→0.820\
    \ (EIDs 1578, 1587, 1586, 1579). A DAQB-like EtC+BRBG baseline rises 0.6928→0.6932 and iLISI 0.581→0.610 for 1024→1280 (EIDs 1574, 1575). Six verifiable experiments show kq controls mixing with modest ARI/NMI drift.\"\nsource_private: 98\n```"
