parts:
- text: "/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Strategic reflection during stagnation: What are the true bottlenecks in our current approach to scRNA integration on Heart, what evidence supports them, and what minimal falsifiable hypotheses should we carry into a new organ to avoid local optima? Define a 3-tier plan (triage, mechanism, publication) that balances mixing (iLISI) versus PCR without collapsing either, and specify verification signals to decide quickly.\ntick: 6\n```\n\nReflection Tick 1/6 — Bottlenecks and evidence\n- Mixing vs PCR trade-off:\n  - Evidence: GraphFWPCA-only consistently collapses mixing and PCR=0 without quotas/BRBG (multiple negatives, iLISI≈0.096, PCR=0.0).\n  - Both-paths unit-variance scaling improved label metrics but collapsed PCR (~0.102) in minimal track; embedding-only neutral/slightly worse; graph-only scaling modestly helped composite without hurting PCR.\n- Topology dependence:\n  - δ increase helps ridge (with BRBG) but harms FWPCA-only;\
    \ σ_kd distributions show small uniformization can be beneficial with BRBG, harmful without.\n- Candidate pool size (kq):\n  - Monotone iLISI gains across baselines; modest runtime/memory slope; robust across architectures but not sufficient to fix PCR when topology is wrong.\n- New SOTA (scale+PBVE lite on plain PCA) suggests:\n  - Feature scale normalization can synergize with PBVE’s per-PC variance equalization; but scaling does not directly port to GraphFWPCA pipelines.\n\nReflection Tick 2/6 — Core hypotheses to carry into a new organ (falsifiable)\n- H1 (Ridge δ): On new organ, δ=0.12 will exceed δ=0.10 on ridge (kd=22, k=50) without harming cLISI/Graph_conn, mirroring Heart.\n  - Pass/Fail signals: +≥0.002 mean score across seeds {0,1} with cLISI ~0.972–0.973 and Graph_conn ~0.968 stable.\n- H2 (kq robustness): Larger kq (≥1024) improves iLISI and composite for equal-quota graphs irrespective of embedding (plain PCA/EtC) with small runtime/mem cost.\n  - Pass/Fail signals: iLISI\
    \ increase monotone (512→1024→1280) while ARI/NMI within ±0.01, PCR not decreasing.\n- H3 (PCR preservation): Graph-only scaling (unit variance per gene before graph PCs) yields ≥+0.003 over unscaled minimal without reducing PCR (≈1.000 normalized).\n  - Pass/Fail signals: PCR remains ≈1.000; iLISI non-decreasing; Graph_conn ≥0.98.\n- H4 (σ_kd predictor): If σ_kd distribution tightens vs Heart, δ↑ benefits ridge; if it widens, δ=0.10 is safer.\n  - Pass/Fail signals: Compare median/CV/Gini of σ_kd; decision rule pre-registered.\n\nReflection Tick 3/6 — Triage protocol for new organ (first 2–4 submissions)\n- T1: Ridge δ sweep: submit δ={0.10, 0.12} with seeds {0,1}. Record 10 metrics and σ_kd summary (global + per-batch). Decide δ default by score and σ_kd rule.\n- T2: Minimal equal-quota baseline: k=50, kq={1024, 1280}, cosine; record iLISI/PCR/Graph_conn; confirm kq monotonicity.\n- T3: (Optional) Graph-only scaling on minimal equal-quota (if kq positive): run λ_r=0.64; verify PCR\
    \ stays ≈1.000 and composite improves.\n- Stop condition: If any branch drops PCR or cLISI substantially, revert to prior default and freeze that branch for this organ.\n\nReflection Tick 4/6 — Mechanism checks and fast diagnostics\n- σ_kd summaries: median/CV/Gini overall and per-batch; confirm directionality vs δ gains.\n- Inbound entropy / directed-gap (if available): detect over-pruning or mode collapse in neighbor sets; expect higher inbound entropy with larger kq and better mixing.\n- Component attribution: when multiple tweaks succeed, flip one at a time (δ, kq, scaling location) to isolate cause; never stack without ablation.\n- Seeds: keep {0,1} for every probe to bound variance; note Cell_cycle as typical spread driver on Heart.\n\nReflection Tick 5/6 — Publication strategy under stagnation\n- Results-only briefs every ~50 ticks:\n  - “Ridge δ generalizes on Organ X” (2–4 EIDs).\n  - “Candidate pool size kq improves mixing on Organ X” (≥2 EIDs).\n  - “Graph-only scaling modestly\
    \ improves minimal composite without PCR loss” (≥2 EIDs).\n- Each brief: full metric vectors, σ_kd deltas, runtime/memory note; no future plans/acks; satisfy generalization by testing across two baselines when feasible.\n- Archive hygiene: tie back to prior Heart conclusions; avoid proposal language.\n\nReflection Tick 6/6 — Long-term direction to avoid local optimum\n- Unify mixing and PCR via principled graph reweighting:\n  - Idea: convex combination of within-batch structural edges (to preserve PCR) and cross-batch quota edges (to ensure mixing), with weights learned from local σ_kd mismatch or batch-proportion priors; tested via simple α sweep with fixed kq.\n  - Guardrails: enforce minimal within-batch degree to prevent PCR collapse; cap cross-batch dominance to maintain label structure.\n- Cross-organ generalization as first-class metric: keep a standing pair of organs (when available) to validate any gain in ≤4 submissions before claiming.\n- Tooling: keep σ_kd/entropy profilers\
    \ and kq cost CSVs in storage; standardize runbooks and metadata to cut iteration time."
thinking_content: '**Structuring improvement proposals**


  I''m considering suggesting a penalty on the L2 difference of PC means across batches to better manage mixing, but I can''t implement that just yet. For scalability and cost-effective enhancements, I''m thinking about kq selection and per-batch candidate budgets to improve mixing efficiency. When a new organ arrives, I''ll propose running a ridge δ sweep and a minimal eq-quota baseline. Lastly, collating reasons why GraphFWPCA-only fails to achieve mixing might clarify how distances collapse affects neighbor interactions and how the mixing gating threshold improves outcomes.**Planning for improvements**


  I''m considering the synergy between PBVE and scaling methods. I might propose a bridging method, applying scaling only in the embedding phase instead of the graph, which previously showed a slight improvement from 0.712 to 0.716. Also, I want to think about a long-term plan that includes expanding our paper pipeline and cross-organ generalization. Creating a "Threats to validity" list and a "Stop doing" list could be helpful. Additionally, I''ll ensure we manage our tokens carefully. Finally, I’ll produce the action for reflection and outline bottlenecks with a 90-day plan.'
