tick: 1195
role: model
parts:
- text: "/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"α_mix sweep: α=0.20, kq=1280 (within‑batch structural + equal‑quota, k=50, cosine)\"\ntags: \"graph, quotas, within-batch, blend, residualization, knn, ablation\"\nabstract: \"Second α_mix ablation to balance PCR and mixing. We blend a within‑batch structural KNN graph (raw PCs) with an equal‑quota cross‑batch graph (batch‑residualized PCs) via C=(1−α)·C_wb+α·C_eq using α=0.20 and a larger candidate pool kq=1280. Distances take the union with elementwise min. Returns both connectivities and distances; neighbors metadata set.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n  import anndata as ad\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from integration_utils import (\n      normalize_log1p_counts, select_features, pca_svd,\n      ridge_residualize_per_pc, equal_quota_knn, set_neighbors_meta\n  )\n\n  def _to_csr(X):\n      return X if sp.isspmatrix_csr(X)\
    \ else (X.tocsr() if sp.issparse(X) else sp.csr_matrix(X))\n\n  def _row_normalize(C):\n      C = _to_csr(C).astype(np.float64)\n      rs = np.asarray(C.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n      rs[rs == 0.0] = 1.0\n      C = C.tocsr(copy=True)\n      C.data *= np.repeat(1.0 / rs, np.diff(C.indptr))\n      return C\n\n  def _build_within_batch_knn(Z, batches, k=50, k_candidates=1280, kwb_target=10, metric=\"cosine\"):\n      n = Z.shape[0]\n      nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=min(k_candidates, n-1) + 1, metric=metric)\n      nn.fit(Z)\n      d_all, idx_all = nn.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True)\n      d = d_all[:, 1:]\n      idx = idx_all[:, 1:]\n      batches = np.asarray(batches)\n\n      rows, cols, wdat, ddat = [], [], [], []\n      for i in range(n):\n          mask = (batches[idx[i]] == batches[i])\n          if mask.any():\n              di = d[i][mask]\n              ji = idx[i][mask]\n              take = min(kwb_target, di.shape[0])\n              if take > 0:\n                  order\
    \ = np.argpartition(di, kth=take-1)[:take]\n                  sel_j = ji[order]\n                  sel_d = di[order]\n                  rows.extend([i]*take)\n                  cols.extend(sel_j.tolist())\n                  wdat.extend([1.0]*take)\n                  ddat.extend(sel_d.tolist())\n      C_dir = sp.coo_matrix((wdat, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      D_dir = sp.coo_matrix((ddat, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      C_sym = C_dir.maximum(C_dir.T)\n      D_sym = D_dir.minimum(D_dir.T)\n      return C_sym.tocsr().astype(np.float64), D_sym.tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n\n  def _union_min_distances(D_a, D_b):\n      D_a = _to_csr(D_a)\n      D_b = _to_csr(D_b)\n      D = D_a.tolil(copy=True)\n      B = D_b.tocsr()\n      for i in range(B.shape[0]):\n          start, end = B.indptr[i], B.indptr[i+1]\n          js = B.indices[start:end]\n          vals = B.data[start:end]\n          if js.size == 0:\n              continue\n          pos = {int(col): p for p, col\
    \ in enumerate(D.rows[i])}\n          new_rows = list(D.rows[i])\n          new_vals = list(D.data[i])\n          updated = False\n          for j, v in zip(js, vals):\n              if j in pos:\n                  p = pos[j]\n                  if v < new_vals[p]:\n                      new_vals[p] = float(v)\n                      updated = True\n              else:\n                  new_rows.append(int(j))\n                  new_vals.append(float(v))\n                  updated = True\n          if updated:\n              D.rows[i] = new_rows\n              D.data[i] = new_vals\n      return D.tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      α_mix graph blend (ablation):\n      - Embedding: PCA60 (log1p-normalized counts) saved to obsm['X_emb'].\n      - Graphs:\n        1) Within-batch structural graph on raw PCs (binary weights), kwb_target≈10.\n        2) Equal-quota cross-batch graph on batch-residualized PCs (cosine),\
    \ k=50, kq=1280.\n      - Blend: C = (1−α)·normalize(C_wb) + α·normalize(C_eq) with α=0.20.\n      - Distances: union with elementwise min over (D_eq, D_wb).\n      \"\"\"\n      # constants\n      K = 50\n      KQ = 1280\n      N_PCS_EMB = 60\n      N_PCS_GRAPH = 50\n      LAMBDA_R = 0.64\n      L2 = 1e-3\n      METRIC = \"cosine\"\n      ALPHA = 0.20\n      KWB_TARGET = max(8, int(round(0.2 * K)))\n\n      # 1) Preprocess + features\n      Xn = normalize_log1p_counts(adata)\n      Xn = select_features(Xn, adata)\n\n      # 2) Embedding\n      Z_emb = pca_svd(Xn, n_components=N_PCS_EMB, random_state=0)\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(Z_emb, dtype=np.float32)\n\n      # 3) Graph PCs\n      Zg_raw = pca_svd(Xn, n_components=N_PCS_GRAPH, random_state=1)\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'])\n\n      # 3a) Within-batch structural graph\n      C_wb, D_wb = _build_within_batch_knn(\n          Zg_raw, batches=batches, k=K, k_candidates=KQ,\n          kwb_target=KWB_TARGET,\
    \ metric=METRIC\n      )\n\n      # 3b) Equal-quota cross-batch graph\n      Zg_corr = ridge_residualize_per_pc(Zg_raw, batches, lam_r=LAMBDA_R, l2=L2)\n      C_eq, D_eq = equal_quota_knn(Zg_corr, batches=batches, k=K, k_candidates=KQ, metric=METRIC)\n\n      # 4) Blend and distances\n      C_blend = (1.0 - ALPHA) * _row_normalize(C_wb) + ALPHA * _row_normalize(C_eq)\n      D_union = _union_min_distances(D_eq, D_wb)\n\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = _to_csr(C_blend).astype(np.float64)\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = _to_csr(D_union).astype(np.float64)\n      set_neighbors_meta(\n          adata, k=K, method='alpha_mix(within_batch + equal_quota)', metric=METRIC,\n          extra={'alpha': ALPHA, 'k_candidates': KQ, 'lam_r': LAMBDA_R, 'kwb_target': KWB_TARGET}\n      )\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating KNN code efficiency**


  I need to ensure "no cell-type usage" is maintained, and it seems I''m on the right track. However, in the within-batch KNN, while using kwb_target neighbors, I forgot to enforce a minimum degree. The current code is set to build it, but what if a node has no neighbors in the same batch? In a large dataset with multiple batches, nearest neighbors might not always be from the same batch. I also need to ensure the efficiency of the distances union function, as looping over rows and writing LIL for 20k nodes could be heavy, but should fit within 30 minutes.**Assessing distance calculations**


  I’m considering that the current method could be costly, especially since distances are on a k-scale. With both C_eq and C_wb being sparse, having around k edges per node, the equality graph also maintains k edges per node while the within-batch may have about 10. I see a possible optimization for the union operation by using element-wise min, but SciPy lacks this feature. Instead, I could combine D_eq and D_wb and adjust for overlaps. The complexity and performance seem acceptable, with a row-normalization needed as well. Plus, I should set neighbor metadata again and manage kwb_target norms. I plan to submit for one additional evaluation while keeping concurrent runs limited to two. Finally, I’ll prepare TE to review the results after everything finishes.**Planning action submission**


  I need to maintain token hygiene while producing the actions. My first step is to execute an action by submitting with new parameters α=0.20 and kq=1280. I can make minimal changes in the code, keeping it almost identical but with those new values. The title and abstract will need to reflect these parameter differences. Storing the blending function for reuse seems optional, but I’ll ensure we’re not exceeding two concurrent runs. I see that we''re at 1700, so I can go ahead and submit one more run. After the submission, I can manage tokens or go into standby if needed. I’ll finalize the action as /execute_action{submit 1} followed by a YAML code block and include a note about combining distances using the "min" union. Let''s proceed!'
