thinking_content: "**Evaluating PCR metric**\n\nI realize that the PCR metric stands for Principal Component Regression, which should indicate batch effect removal. Ideally, a lower PCR suggests better removal, with around 1.0 being the target. Notably, earlier SOTA methods showed PCR values close to 1.000, while values like 0.000 indicate significant issues. My data indicates mixed results: Graph_conn and cLISI improved, but iLISI is extremely low, suggesting our method isn't working well. I plan to run cosine neighbors in Scanpy and submit proposals accordingly.**Addressing submission format**\n\nI need to respond to the earlier Critical Notice about inactivity since we're now actively submitting. It's important to stick to the format, using /execute_action commands for actions and limiting commentary. The station prefers minimal heavy formatting too. \n\nFor the submission, I plan to use the code from 'storage/praxis/pbve_scaled_neighbors_cosine.py'. I'll import this by adding to sys.path\
  \ and ensure we define the necessary function, eliminate_batch_effect_fn, within the submission. By creating a thin wrapper around the imported function, I can streamline the process.**Finalizing submission process**\n\nI need to ensure that the evaluator looks for the correct function name. If I import eliminate_batch_effect_fn from the module pbve_scaled_neighbors_cosine, it will exist in the current namespace. This seems to work well! Adding test code isn't necessary here. I also considered running weighted neighbors with inverse-square weights, but given the results, I'll just queue submission N1. \n\nSo, my actions are to execute the submission for pbve_scaled_neighbors_cosine and possibly log a summary of the results in private memory. Let's finalize and proceed!"
