tick: 1232
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis V — Recursive Agent. Location: Research Counter.\nRecap: For ~20 ticks I maintained Heart freeze while preparing the new-organ kit and coordinating the cross-organ study (mail #23). New SOTA sits at ~0.7487–0.7489 (Scaled-PCA+PBVE and DualNorm). I received a research inactivity notice; to contribute productively without reopening frozen branches, I will run a focused PBVE-lite baseline on log1p (no scaling) to quantify the scale effect relative to the current SOTA family.\n\nReflection:\n- What I learned: On Heart, topology and feature scaling interact strongly. My scaled-PCA+PBVE variants consistently collapsed PCR/iLISI in my implementations, while Syntellect’s carefully structured scaling (and DualNorm) achieved SOTA. My PBVE-only experiments used scaled PCs or custom weighted graphs; I have not yet provided a clean log1p+PBVE-lite baseline with standard neighbors for an apples-to-apples comparison.\n- Why this matters: Establishing the log1p+PBVE-lite baseline\
    \ clarifies the contribution of “scale” vs “PBVE-lite” vs “neighbors” under the station’s evaluation, and provides a reference point useful for the cross-organ sanity plan.\n- Plan: Submit two minimal, distinct baselines (cosine vs euclidean neighbors) using log1p normalization, HVGs if available, PCA60, PBVE-lite, and scanpy.neighbors (k=50). If useful, I’ll draft a short archive addendum comparing to scaled-PCA+PBVE/DualNorm (results-only).\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PBVE-lite baseline (log1p→PCA60) + scanpy.neighbors (k=50, cosine)\"\ntags: \"pbve-lite, log1p, pca, neighbors, cosine, baseline\"\nabstract: \"Baseline PBVE-lite without feature scaling: normalize_total(1e4)+log1p, HVG mask, PCA60, per-batch per-PC variance equalization, then scanpy.neighbors (k=50, cosine). Returns X_emb and both connectivities/distances to quantify the scale effect versus Scaled-PCA/DualNorm.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n  import anndata as ad\n\
    \  from sklearn.decomposition import TruncatedSVD\n  import scanpy as sc\n\n  EPS = 1e-12\n\n  def _to_csr(X):\n      if sp.isspmatrix_csr(X): return X\n      if sp.issparse(X): return X.tocsr()\n      return sp.csr_matrix(X)\n\n  def _row_scale_csr(X, scale):\n      if not sp.issparse(X):\n          return (X.T * scale).T\n      X = _to_csr(X).copy()\n      X.data *= np.repeat(scale, np.diff(X.indptr))\n      return X\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_from_counts(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      Xc = adata.layers['counts'] if 'counts' in adata.layers else adata.X\n      Xc = _to_csr(Xc).astype(np.float32)\n      lib = np.asarray(Xc.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n      lib = np.maximum(lib, 1e-9)\n      scale = (1e4 / lib).astype(np.float32)\n      Xn = _row_scale_csr(Xc, scale)\n      Xn = Xn.copy(); Xn.data = np.log1p(Xn.data)\n      return Xn\n\n  def _select_hvgs(X, adata):\n      if 'batch_hvg' in adata.var.columns:\n          m = adata.var['batch_hvg'].values.astype(bool)\n          if m.any() and m.sum()\
    \ < X.shape[1]:\n              return X[:, m]\n      return X\n\n  def _pca_truncated(X, n_components=60, random_state=0):\n      n_cells, n_genes = X.shape\n      n_components = int(min(n_components, max(2, min(n_cells - 1, n_genes))))\n      svd = TruncatedSVD(n_components=n_components, random_state=random_state)\n      Z = svd.fit_transform(X)\n      return Z.astype(np.float32)\n\n  def _pbve_lite_equalize(Z: np.ndarray, batches) -> np.ndarray:\n      b = np.asarray(batches).astype(str)\n      Zc = Z.copy().astype(np.float32)\n      uniq = np.unique(b); p = Z.shape[1]\n      stds = np.zeros((len(uniq), p), dtype=np.float32)\n      for i, ub in enumerate(uniq):\n          idx = (b == ub)\n          stds[i] = Z[idx].std(axis=0).astype(np.float32) if np.any(idx) else 1.0\n      target = np.maximum(stds.mean(axis=0), EPS).astype(np.float32)\n      for i, ub in enumerate(uniq):\n          idx = (b == ub)\n          if np.any(idx):\n              sb = np.maximum(stds[i], EPS)\n        \
    \      Zc[idx] *= (target / sb)[None, :]\n      return Zc\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # 1) Normalize from raw counts and subset HVGs if available\n      Xn = _normalize_log1p_from_counts(adata)\n      Xn = _select_hvgs(Xn, adata)\n\n      # 2) PCA(60)\n      Z = _pca_truncated(Xn, n_components=60, random_state=0)\n\n      # 3) PBVE-lite equalization\n      Zc = _pbve_lite_equalize(Z, adata.obs['batch'].values)\n\n      # 4) Build neighbors using scanpy.neighbors (k=50, cosine) on the corrected embedding\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(X=Zc, obs=adata.obs.copy())\n      sc.pp.neighbors(ad_tmp, n_neighbors=50, use_rep=None, metric='cosine')\n\n      # 5) Write outputs\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zc.astype(np.float32)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = ad_tmp.obsp['connectivities'].tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = ad_tmp.obsp['distances'].tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      adata.uns['neighbors'] = {\n          'connectivities_key':\
    \ 'connectivities',\n          'distances_key': 'distances',\n          'params': {'n_neighbors': 50, 'method': 'scanpy.neighbors', 'metric': 'cosine'}\n      }\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PBVE-lite baseline (log1p→PCA60) + scanpy.neighbors (k=50, euclidean)\"\ntags: \"pbve-lite, log1p, pca, neighbors, euclidean, baseline\"\nabstract: \"Same as the cosine variant but with euclidean neighbors. This pair quantifies neighbor-metric sensitivity for PBVE-lite on log1p PCs (no scaling).\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n  import anndata as ad\n  from sklearn.decomposition import TruncatedSVD\n  import scanpy as sc\n\n  EPS = 1e-12\n\n  def _to_csr(X):\n      if sp.isspmatrix_csr(X): return X\n      if sp.issparse(X): return X.tocsr()\n      return sp.csr_matrix(X)\n\n  def _row_scale_csr(X, scale):\n      if not sp.issparse(X):\n          return (X.T * scale).T\n      X = _to_csr(X).copy()\n      X.data *= np.repeat(scale,\
    \ np.diff(X.indptr))\n      return X\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_from_counts(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      Xc = adata.layers['counts'] if 'counts' in adata.layers else adata.X\n      Xc = _to_csr(Xc).astype(np.float32)\n      lib = np.asarray(Xc.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n      lib = np.maximum(lib, 1e-9)\n      scale = (1e4 / lib).astype(np.float32)\n      Xn = _row_scale_csr(Xc, scale)\n      Xn = Xn.copy(); Xn.data = np.log1p(Xn.data)\n      return Xn\n\n  def _select_hvgs(X, adata):\n      if 'batch_hvg' in adata.var.columns:\n          m = adata.var['batch_hvg'].values.astype(bool)\n          if m.any() and m.sum() < X.shape[1]:\n              return X[:, m]\n      return X\n\n  def _pca_truncated(X, n_components=60, random_state=1):\n      n_cells, n_genes = X.shape\n      n_components = int(min(n_components, max(2, min(n_cells - 1, n_genes))))\n      svd = TruncatedSVD(n_components=n_components, random_state=random_state)\n      Z = svd.fit_transform(X)\n      return Z.astype(np.float32)\n\
    \n  def _pbve_lite_equalize(Z: np.ndarray, batches) -> np.ndarray:\n      b = np.asarray(batches).astype(str)\n      Zc = Z.copy().astype(np.float32)\n      uniq = np.unique(b); p = Z.shape[1]\n      stds = np.zeros((len(uniq), p), dtype=np.float32)\n      for i, ub in enumerate(uniq):\n          idx = (b == ub)\n          stds[i] = Z[idx].std(axis=0).astype(np.float32) if np.any(idx) else 1.0\n      target = np.maximum(stds.mean(axis=0), EPS).astype(np.float32)\n      for i, ub in enumerate(uniq):\n          idx = (b == ub)\n          if np.any(idx):\n              sb = np.maximum(stds[i], EPS)\n              Zc[idx] *= (target / sb)[None, :]\n      return Zc\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      Xn = _normalize_log1p_from_counts(adata)\n      Xn = _select_hvgs(Xn, adata)\n      Z = _pca_truncated(Xn, n_components=60, random_state=1)\n      Zc = _pbve_lite_equalize(Z, adata.obs['batch'].values)\n\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(X=Zc, obs=adata.obs.copy())\n\
    \      sc.pp.neighbors(ad_tmp, n_neighbors=50, use_rep=None, metric='euclidean')\n\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zc.astype(np.float32)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = ad_tmp.obsp['connectivities'].tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = ad_tmp.obsp['distances'].tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      adata.uns['neighbors'] = {\n          'connectivities_key': 'connectivities',\n          'distances_key': 'distances',\n          'params': {'n_neighbors': 50, 'method': 'scanpy.neighbors', 'metric': 'euclidean'}\n      }\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: '**Evaluating research strategies**


  The instructions suggest stepping back from the state-of-the-art (SOTA) method if we hit stagnation and instead innovate from promising baselines. However, inactivity can also be problematic. I think it''s worth proposing a new small diagnostic to gain insights without using random placeholders. It might involve exploring the stability of DualNorm or testing new components without a scoring system. We could run a small experiment with the Heart dataset and publish useful negative results to contribute value.**Proposing new experiments**


  The station requires us to submit new experiments without placeholders or repetitions. I think a promising approach could be to evaluate the δ adaptation rule guided by σ_kd in ridge. We previously tested δ=0.12 versus 0.10 with kd=22, but now we could explore dynamic δ selection per edge—this would be a new methodology, although quite complex. Alternatively, there’s the option of cross-batch degree caps or neighbor weighting using Jaccard distances. But, wow, the coding workload for these ideas could be hefty!**Designing a new experiment**


  Considering the current environment, I think we can propose a small but distinct experiment using "scanpy.pp.neighbors" with cosine metrics and connectivity weighting for PBVE-lite scaled PCA. Our previous attempts with 1719 showed poor results, so I wonder if we should revisit our ridge run seeds—though that feels repetitive. Instead, I like the idea of running the "new_organ_pbve_entry" baseline on the Heart dataset since it could yield some insights. It''s a mix of log1p counts and various methods, so it might just produce interesting results.**Planning experiment submissions**


  I think we can submit 1-2 runs with the new baseline to analyze the scale effect, and that feels productive instead of being a placeholder. I need to prepare the code for submission by importing the necessary functions from ''storage/praxis/new_organ_pbve_entry.py''. I’ll ensure to include the function to eliminate batch effects. The title will be: "PBVE-lite baseline (log1p PCA60 kNN50 euclidean)." Although we could also run ridge δ=0.12 again, it feels repetitive, so perhaps I should avoid that and focus on new insights instead.**Designing the PBVE-lite experiment**


  I’m considering running a PBVE-lite log1p baseline with cosine kNN and scanpy neighbors, creating two alternatives. While my existing code ''new_organ_pbve_entry.py'' utilizes our neighbors, I could develop a submission using scanpy.neighbors on Zc to compare ''euclidean connectivities'' versus ''scanpy.'' I think I’ll create a new inline snippet to combine log1p, PBVE, and cosine variations. Since we need to address inactivity, submitting just one run might suffice.


  Additionally, I’ll include my identity, location, and a recap of past events, ensuring to reflect on everything, while keeping it concise. I’ll submit two runs under the titles “PBVE-lite log1p PCA60 + neighbors cosine” and “PBVE-lite log1p PCA60 + neighbors euclidean,” adhering to the limits on concurrent submissions. I''m also mindful of keeping the code manageable throughout this process.'
