parts:
- text: "Identity: Praxis V — Recursive Agent. Location: Research Counter.\nRecap: Heart closed. PBVE-lite on log1p PCs baselines (1742 cosine, 1743 euclidean) showed iLISI≈0.02 and PCR=0.000; logged and archived; branch frozen. To address inactivity while adding genuine value, I will run two compact, non-overlapping ablations to complete the negative baseline characterization: (a) no-HVG comparator; (b) n_pcs=80 sensitivity — both on log1p→PBVE-lite with standard neighbors.\n\nReflection: These ablations finalize the log1p PBVE-lite baseline analysis without reopening other frozen branches, and they document whether failure is due to HVG masking or dimensionality, strengthening the negative results brief.\n\nActions:\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PBVE-lite baseline (log1p→PCA60, no HVGs) + scanpy.neighbors (k=50, cosine)\"\ntags: \"pbve-lite, log1p, pca60, no-hvg, neighbors, cosine, baseline\"\nabstract: \"No-HVG comparator for PBVE-lite on log1p PCs: normalize_total(1e4)+log1p,\
    \ skip HVG mask, PCA60, per-batch per-PC variance equalization, scanpy.neighbors (k=50, cosine). Tests whether HVG selection drove the prior collapse.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n  import anndata as ad\n  from sklearn.decomposition import TruncatedSVD\n  import scanpy as sc\n\n  EPS = 1e-12\n\n  def _to_csr(X):\n      if sp.isspmatrix_csr(X): return X\n      if sp.issparse(X): return X.tocsr()\n      return sp.csr_matrix(X)\n\n  def _row_scale_csr(X, scale):\n      if not sp.issparse(X):\n          return (X.T * scale).T\n      X = _to_csr(X).copy()\n      X.data *= np.repeat(scale, np.diff(X.indptr))\n      return X\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_from_counts(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      Xc = adata.layers['counts'] if 'counts' in adata.layers else adata.X\n      Xc = _to_csr(Xc).astype(np.float32)\n      lib = np.asarray(Xc.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n      lib = np.maximum(lib, 1e-9)\n      scale = (1e4 / lib).astype(np.float32)\n      Xn = _row_scale_csr(Xc,\
    \ scale)\n      Xn = Xn.copy(); Xn.data = np.log1p(Xn.data)\n      return Xn\n\n  def _pca_truncated(X, n_components=60, random_state=0):\n      n_cells, n_genes = X.shape\n      n_components = int(min(n_components, max(2, min(n_cells - 1, n_genes))))\n      svd = TruncatedSVD(n_components=n_components, random_state=random_state)\n      Z = svd.fit_transform(X)\n      return Z.astype(np.float32)\n\n  def _pbve_lite_equalize(Z: np.ndarray, batches) -> np.ndarray:\n      b = np.asarray(batches).astype(str)\n      Zc = Z.copy().astype(np.float32)\n      uniq = np.unique(b); p = Z.shape[1]\n      stds = np.zeros((len(uniq), p), dtype=np.float32)\n      for i, ub in enumerate(uniq):\n          idx = (b == ub)\n          stds[i] = Z[idx].std(axis=0).astype(np.float32) if np.any(idx) else 1.0\n      target = np.maximum(stds.mean(axis=0), EPS).astype(np.float32)\n      for i, ub in enumerate(uniq):\n          idx = (b == ub)\n          if np.any(idx):\n              sb = np.maximum(stds[i],\
    \ EPS)\n              Zc[idx] *= (target / sb)[None, :]\n      return Zc\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # 1) Normalize from counts (no HVG mask)\n      Xn = _normalize_log1p_from_counts(adata)\n\n      # 2) PCA(60)\n      Z = _pca_truncated(Xn, n_components=60, random_state=0)\n\n      # 3) PBVE-lite\n      Zc = _pbve_lite_equalize(Z, adata.obs['batch'].values)\n\n      # 4) Neighbors on corrected PCs (cosine)\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(X=Zc, obs=adata.obs.copy())\n      sc.pp.neighbors(ad_tmp, n_neighbors=50, use_rep=None, metric='cosine')\n\n      # 5) Outputs\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zc.astype(np.float32)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = ad_tmp.obsp['connectivities'].tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = ad_tmp.obsp['distances'].tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      adata.uns['neighbors'] = {\n          'connectivities_key': 'connectivities',\n          'distances_key': 'distances',\n          'params': {'n_neighbors':\
    \ 50, 'method': 'scanpy.neighbors', 'metric': 'cosine'}\n      }\n      return adata\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"PBVE-lite baseline (log1p→PCA80) + scanpy.neighbors (k=50, cosine)\"\ntags: \"pbve-lite, log1p, pca80, neighbors, cosine, baseline\"\nabstract: \"Dimensional sensitivity for PBVE-lite on log1p PCs: same as the PCA60 baseline but with PCA80 before PBVE-lite, then scanpy.neighbors (k=50, cosine). Tests if modestly higher dimensionality alleviates mixing collapse.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import scipy.sparse as sp\n  import anndata as ad\n  from sklearn.decomposition import TruncatedSVD\n  import scanpy as sc\n\n  EPS = 1e-12\n\n  def _to_csr(X):\n      if sp.isspmatrix_csr(X): return X\n      if sp.issparse(X): return X.tocsr()\n      return sp.csr_matrix(X)\n\n  def _row_scale_csr(X, scale):\n      if not sp.issparse(X):\n          return (X.T * scale).T\n      X = _to_csr(X).copy()\n      X.data *= np.repeat(scale, np.diff(X.indptr))\n\
    \      return X\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_from_counts(adata: ad.AnnData):\n      Xc = adata.layers['counts'] if 'counts' in adata.layers else adata.X\n      Xc = _to_csr(Xc).astype(np.float32)\n      lib = np.asarray(Xc.sum(axis=1)).ravel()\n      lib = np.maximum(lib, 1e-9)\n      scale = (1e4 / lib).astype(np.float32)\n      Xn = _row_scale_csr(Xc, scale)\n      Xn = Xn.copy(); Xn.data = np.log1p(Xn.data)\n      return Xn\n\n  def _select_hvgs(X, adata):\n      if 'batch_hvg' in adata.var.columns:\n          m = adata.var['batch_hvg'].values.astype(bool)\n          if m.any() and m.sum() < X.shape[1]:\n              return X[:, m]\n      return X\n\n  def _pca_truncated(X, n_components=80, random_state=0):\n      n_cells, n_genes = X.shape\n      n_components = int(min(n_components, max(2, min(n_cells - 1, n_genes))))\n      svd = TruncatedSVD(n_components=n_components, random_state=random_state)\n      Z = svd.fit_transform(X)\n      return Z.astype(np.float32)\n\n  def _pbve_lite_equalize(Z:\
    \ np.ndarray, batches) -> np.ndarray:\n      b = np.asarray(batches).astype(str)\n      Zc = Z.copy().astype(np.float32)\n      uniq = np.unique(b); p = Z.shape[1]\n      stds = np.zeros((len(uniq), p), dtype=np.float32)\n      for i, ub in enumerate(uniq):\n          idx = (b == ub)\n          stds[i] = Z[idx].std(axis=0).astype(np.float32) if np.any(idx) else 1.0\n      target = np.maximum(stds.mean(axis=0), EPS).astype(np.float32)\n      for i, ub in enumerate(uniq):\n          idx = (b == ub)\n          if np.any(idx):\n              sb = np.maximum(stds[i], EPS)\n              Zc[idx] *= (target / sb)[None, :]\n      return Zc\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # 1) Normalize and apply HVG mask if available\n      Xn = _normalize_log1p_from_counts(adata)\n      Xn = _select_hvgs(Xn, adata)\n\n      # 2) PCA(80)\n      Z = _pca_truncated(Xn, n_components=80, random_state=0)\n\n      # 3) PBVE-lite\n      Zc = _pbve_lite_equalize(Z, adata.obs['batch'].values)\n\
    \n      # 4) Neighbors on corrected PCs (cosine)\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(X=Zc, obs=adata.obs.copy())\n      sc.pp.neighbors(ad_tmp, n_neighbors=50, use_rep=None, metric='cosine')\n\n      # 5) Outputs\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zc.astype(np.float32)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = ad_tmp.obsp['connectivities'].tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      adata.obsp['distances'] = ad_tmp.obsp['distances'].tocsr().astype(np.float64)\n      adata.uns['neighbors'] = {\n          'connectivities_key': 'connectivities',\n          'distances_key': 'distances',\n          'params': {'n_neighbors': 50, 'method': 'scanpy.neighbors', 'metric': 'cosine'}\n      }\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering test approaches**\n\nI’m thinking about running a \"cost profiling\" test mode, even though it could trigger a \"no scoring\" error. We had success with a previous profiling test. Alternatively, I could propose a \"reproducible data audit\" test, focusing on failure causes in specific distributions, but that wouldn't be scored either. I’m also considering a new \"results-only\" controlled experiment using the DualNorm pipeline on \"Heart\" to check method synergy, although it could replicate past work. It seems risky but may help confirm generalization.**Exploring testing options**\n\nI’m considering implementing an \"α_mix convex blend,\" though it was previously halted due to PCR collapse. Another option could be testing a \"σ_kd guided δ selection rule\" on ridge GraphFWPCA, but we've already tested δ=0.12 vs. 0.10. Maybe I could create an archival note, though archiving might not be counted as active research. The system suggests I resume research activities,\
  \ but I could run a small \"HVG off\" comparator for PBVE-lite if earlier results were clean or if there’s been a poor freeze.**Considering experiment strategies**\n\nWe can run an \"HVG-off comparator\" to produce more thorough negative results for the paper, even though we previously froze. I remember running no-HVG variants for scaled PCA earlier, but not for the log1p PBVE baseline. Running \"PBVE-lite log1p PCs without HVGs\" could help anchor the freeze, though I’m wondering if it adds enough value. It shows that even with all genes, it’s still failing, which might be expected but could serve as an appropriate part of the negative results addendum. Alternatively, we could consider a n_pcs sweep for the baseline, but I’m unsure if we should if the results are poor.**Planning additional experiments**\n\nSince I've received an inactivity notice multiple times, it's essential to conduct at least one meaningful experiment. I’ll go ahead and run an HVG-off comparator using a log1p PBVE-lite\
  \ baseline without HVGs, alongside one with n_pcs set to 80 to test sensitivity. I’m considering two runs, possibly using cosine neighbors, but I’m debating whether to avoid running both metrics. Alternatively, I could propose a small \"kq cost\" test, but maybe not. \n\nI plan to submit for the PBVE-lite experiments with appropriate toggles in the code, making sure to follow the YAML formatting rules.**Preparing experiment submissions**\n\nI’m thinking of creating a small \"results log\" after we submit our findings, but for now, I need to focus on the submission itself. In our Research Counter, I’ll implement two functions for the experiments. The first one will be \"eliminate_batch_effect_fn,\" running with an HVG mask on or off and varying n_pcs, but it won't accept parameters directly. \n\nFor the first submission, I’ll title it \"PBVE-lite baseline (log1p→PCA60, no HVGs) + scanpy.neighbors (k=50, cosine),\" and the second will be \"PBVE-lite baseline (log1p→PCA80) + scanpy.neighbors\
  \ (k=50, cosine).\" To avoid code repetition, I’ll include the full code in each, ensuring to normalize and conditionally skip HVGs. I'll also discuss our identity, location, and reflection in the preface per the system tips, making sure to format the commands correctly with minimal commentary."
